log_irc5_0042,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1042,2025-05-07T06:27:52Z,2025-05-07T06:28:52Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,pm_gearbox_oil_change,maintenance,PM,completed,10,INFO,comp_gearboxes,GBX Axis 1 (reducer),,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1042,"PM: Axis 1 gearbox oil change — clean oil, normal run","Performed scheduled gearbox oil change on IRC5 Axis 1 gearbox. Drained 150 mL of Mobilgear 600 XP 220; oil appeared clear amber with no contamination and no metal flakes on magnetic drain plug. Housing temp at start 42°C, end-of-service temp 38°C. After refill to sight glass level, cycled axis at 20% speed for 10 cycles; no abnormal noise or torque deviation observed.","Isolated power, removed drain plug and collected oil sample (labelled A1-20250507). Cleaned and reinstalled magnetic drain plug, torqued to 12 Nm. Refilled with Mobilgear 600 XP 220 to specified level, bled air, cycled axis at low speed, logged run test in CMMS.",resolved,225,177,229,IRC5 gearbox axis1 Mobilgear600XP220 drained_qty=150mL metal_particles=none temp_start=42C cycled axis; sample logged,irc5_pm_gearbox_oil_change,,sym_gearbox_axis_6_getting_hot,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0035,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1035,2025-05-20T16:06:44Z,2025-05-20T16:07:44Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,pm_brake_inspection,maintenance,PM,completed,10,INFO,comp_robot_brakes,IRC5 holding brakes (axes 1-6),,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1035,PM brake inspection — axes 1-6 visual and functional check,"Performed scheduled PM inspection of holding brakes on IRC5 axes 1–6. Covers removed, visual check found clean rotors and no contamination; lining thickness averaged 2.5 mm across axes. Coil supply measured 24.0 V DC and brake release test (controller routine) showed release time ~120 ms with holding torque within spec. No action required beyond cleaning and result logging.","Removed arm covers, wiped brake faces with lint-free cloth, measured lining thickness with calipers, verified coil voltage (24.0 VDC) and ran ABB brake test routine; reassembled covers and updated maintenance log.",resolved,177,105,177,"IRC5, Robot brakes, visual inspection, lining thickness 2.5mm, coil voltage 24.0V, release time 120ms, brake test pass, cleaned and logged",irc5_pm_brake_inspection,,sym_holding_brakes_non_operational,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0036,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1036,2025-06-08T10:41:25Z,2025-06-08T10:43:25Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,pm_brake_inspection,maintenance,PM,completed,10,INFO,comp_robot_brakes,Axis 2 brake (IRC5),,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1036,Brake PM — Axis 2 surface condition and functional check,Scheduled inspection of Axis 2 brake assembly. Noted light surface staining/rust on outer rotor but no scoring; lining thickness measured 2.1 mm which is within replacement limits. Brake engage/release cycle executed successfully with no chatter and normal holding torque. Cleaned contact surfaces and documented findings.,"Opened access panel, removed rotor dust cover, wire-brushed light rust from rotor, measured lining with digital caliper, ran single-axis brake engage test and resealed cover; logged inspection notes.",resolved,175,129,125,"IRC5, Axis 2 brake, visual rust light, lining thickness 2.1mm, brake engage/release OK, cleaned rotor, inspection logged",irc5_pm_brake_inspection,,,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0037,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1037,2025-08-04T15:14:49Z,2025-08-04T15:14:49Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,pm_brake_inspection,maintenance,PM,completed,10,INFO,comp_robot_brakes,Axis 3 brake assembly,,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1037,PM brake inspection — Axis 3 wear flagged for next cycle,Preventive check on Axis 3 revealed reduced lining thickness at 1.4 mm (service limit 1.5 mm); minor increase in release time observed (220 ms) though brake still holds in static test. No debris or oil contamination found on rotor; wear consistent with service life. Flagged brake for replacement on next PM cycle and ordered spare lining kit.,"Measured lining thickness with caliper (1.4 mm), ran controlled brake torque check, attached yellow 'replace at next PM' tag to axis 3 motor housing, logged part order for brake kit, tightened electrical connector to coil and reassembled.",resolved,147,197,158,"IRC5, Axis 3 brake, lining thickness 1.4mm (below limit), release time 220ms, brake torque test OK, tagged for replacement and ordered parts",irc5_pm_brake_inspection,,sym_holding_brakes_non_operational,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0001,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1001,2025-09-07T10:46:58Z,2025-09-07T10:47:58Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,ethernet_packet_loss,alarm,CM,closed,18,ERROR,comp_communications,Communications - Ethernet trunk (IRC5 → PLC),1001,,ethernet_packet_loss_pct,11.08,%,2.0,%,WO-IRC5-1001,High packet loss — damaged CAT6A trunk cable found,Intermittent drops observed during production; ethernet_packet_loss_pct peaked at 11.08%. Technician inspected cable run from IRC5 to PLC and found visible outer jacket abrasion and exposed pairs at a 90° kink near floor tray (continuity to shield poor). Controller link was flapping (LINK=DOWN intermittently) and flexpendant comms lagging during faults.,"Isolated network, removed damaged CAT6A trunk, installed new shielded CAT6A, terminated with industrial RJ45, verified cable mapping and continuity (pin-to-pin OK, shield continuity 0.2Ω). Reconnected IRC5 → PLC, verified ping 1ms average, packet loss reduced to 0.2% over 30 min burn; logs clear. Closed.",resolved,94,152,68,IRC5 Communications ethernet damaged-cable CAT6A ethernet_packet_loss_pct=11.08% action: replaced CAT6A trunk; controller link flapping,irc5_communications_ethernet_packet_loss,fm_flexpendant_cable_damaged,,link_replaced_by_embedding,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0008,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1008,2025-09-20T17:11:23Z,2025-09-20T17:12:23Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,flexpendant_disconnected,alarm,CM,closed,14,WARN,,FlexPendant (teach pendant),,,flexpendant_connected,0,boolean,1,boolean,WO-IRC5-1008,FlexPendant went black intermittently; power LED off — reseated connector,Operator reported pendant screen going black during a cycle; pendant power LED was dark and flexpendant_connected flagged 0 on the IRC5. Visual inspection showed RJ45 at the pendant slightly working loose and the strain relief screw on the boot was not seated. After reseating the RJ45 and tightening strain relief the pendant regained connection and remained stable for the next 30 min under test.,"1) Isolated cell, powered down pendant circuit. 2) Removed pendant boot, inspected RJ45 plug — pins straight, connector fully reseated into DSQC-662 port. 3) Tightened strain relief screw on pendant boot and routed cable to relieve tension. 4) Measured DSQC-662 DC feed = 24.0 V DC at plug. 5) Powered up, cleared alarm, verified flexpendant_connected=1 and 15 min functional test.",resolved,57,66,26,"IRC5 FlexPendant power LED off, flexpendant_connected=0, RJ45 loose, tighten strain relief, 24.0V measured, reseat connector",irc5_flexpendant_disconnected,fm_flexpendant_power_supply_from_controller_faulty,sym_flexpendant_starts_but_does_not_communicate_with_controller,"missing_component,link_replaced_by_embedding","{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0043,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1043,2025-09-22T12:26:33Z,2025-09-22T12:29:33Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,pm_gearbox_oil_change,maintenance,PM,completed,10,INFO,comp_gearboxes,Gearbox Axis 2 (planetary GBX-A2),,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1043,"PM: Axis 2 gearbox oil change — slight discoloration, fine metal traces",Scheduled oil change on IRC5 Axis 2 gearbox completed. Drained ~180 mL Shell Omala 220; oil showed light brown discoloration and small ferrous fines were collected on the magnetic drain plug (approx. 0.25 g). No evidence of leaking seals or excessive play in gearbox; housing temp stable at 47°C. Sent oil sample and magnetic debris to maintenance lab for ferrous particle analysis and logged recommendation for follow-up inspection of backlash in two weeks.,"Powered down robot, drained gearbox into clean container, removed and weighed magnetic drain plug debris (0.25 g), cleaned drain magnet and reinstall (torque 14 Nm). Refilled with Shell Omala 220 to sight mark, ran axis at 10% for 5 minutes and 30% for 2 minutes, recorded vibration baseline and submitted sample to lab (job# LAB-20250922-A2).",resolved,208,252,214,IRC5 gearbox axis2 ShellOmala220 drained_qty=180mL metal_particles=fine 0.25g temp=47C sample_sent; debris weighed,irc5_pm_gearbox_oil_change,fm_gearbox_oil_too_hot,sym_oil_and_grease_stains_on_motors_and_gearboxes,link_inferred_by_embedding,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0038,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1038,2025-10-02T07:07:20Z,2025-10-02T07:08:20Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,pm_brake_inspection,maintenance,PM,completed,10,INFO,comp_robot_brakes,IRC5 brake coils (axes 4-6),,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1038,Brake coils and connections check — axes 4-6,Inspected brake coils and electrical connections for axes 4–6. Coil resistance measured 28.5 Ω on average and coil supply under load 23.8 V DC; no intermittent continuity seen. Functional brake test completed with normal timing and no abnormal noise. Cleared connectors and secured harness clamps.,"Opened controller cover, probed coil resistance and supply voltage, exercised brakes via teach pendant test, cleaned connector contacts with contact cleaner, secured tie-downs and updated electrical check sheet.",resolved,180,197,166,"IRC5, Brake coils axes4-6, coil R 28.5Ω, coil V 23.8V, continuity OK, brake test OK, cleaned connectors",irc5_pm_brake_inspection,,sym_holding_brakes_non_operational,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0002,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1002,2025-10-19T15:08:59Z,2025-10-19T15:11:59Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,ethernet_packet_loss,alarm,CM,closed,18,ERROR,comp_communications,Patch cable / RJ45 wall jack (IRC5 side),,,ethernet_packet_loss_pct,7.4,%,2.0,%,WO-IRC5-1002,Packet loss traced to loose RJ45 plug at wall jack,"7.4% packet loss measured while robot in cycle. Found RJ45 plug at wall jack for IRC5 patch panel loose with intermittent contact on pins 1/2; RJ45 housing cracked, contact bounce observed when wiggled. Controller link remained UP but flapped with micro-drops; AXC LED on I/O rack was steady green (AXC OK).","Shutdown PLC comms, pulled patch lead, cut back and re-terminated with new industrial RJ45 plug, applied heat-shrink strain relief and secured cable clip. Restored link, monitored 45 minutes: packet loss dropped to 0.1% and ping jitter normal. Documented location and installed label.",resolved,100,168,23,IRC5 Communications patch-RJ45 ethernet_packet_loss_pct=7.40% action: re-terminated RJ45 plug; loose plug caused intermittent contact,irc5_communications_ethernet_packet_loss,fm_ethernet_network_has_problems,,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0020,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1020,2025-10-27T18:21:36Z,2025-10-27T18:25:36Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,brake_release_fault,fault,CM,closed,18,ERROR,comp_robot_brakes,Axis 2 holding brake (coil & connector),,,brake_release_voltage_vdc,21.48,VDC,24,VDC,WO-IRC5-1020,Axis 2 holding brake release voltage low; connector cleaned and retested,"During commanded move the arm would not release Axis 2 holding brake; measured brake_release_voltage_vdc = 21.48 VDC (threshold 24 VDC). Secured arm with a rated mechanical restraint strap before work, verified cable continuity (0.8 Ω) and measured brake coil resistance 48.3 Ω. Found corrosion and a loose spade terminal at the brake connector; cleaned contacts, re-crimped the terminal and tightened the connector. After reassembly voltage rose to 24.1 VDC and normal brake release was confirmed under test motion.","Secured arm with mechanical strap; disabled servo power; measured VDC at TB12 (21.48 V), measured coil R = 48.3 Ω; cleaned corroded spade terminal, re-crimped and re-seated connector; tightened terminal screws; cycled power and verified brake_release_voltage = 24.1 V and axis released; removed restraint and returned to service.",resolved,191,177,132,IRC5 Axis 2 holding brake; brake_release_voltage_vdc=21.48V; coil_res=48.3Ω; continuity=0.8Ω; cleaned/tightened connector; secured arm,irc5_brake_release_fault,fm_holding_brakes_disconnected_faulty_worn_or_non_operational,sym_holding_brakes_non_operational,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0003,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1003,2025-11-01T06:02:56Z,2025-11-01T06:06:56Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,ethernet_packet_loss,alarm,CM,closed,14,WARN,comp_communications,Network Switch - Port 12 (PLC VLAN),1103,,ethernet_packet_loss_pct,6.5,%,2.0,%,WO-IRC5-1003,Switch port fault — port 12 producing CRC/frame errors,"Elevated packet loss (6.5%) while robot running. Examined network switch (Cisco Catalyst, rack 2) and observed Port 12 LED amber with error counters (CRC/frame errors increasing). IRC5 controller link showed intermittent Rx errors; flexpendant occasionally lost controller comms.","Moved IRC5 uplink to spare port 24, disabled Energy-Efficient Ethernet on that port, power-cycled switch module. Observed error counters stop incrementing on new port; packet loss fell to 0.3% after migration and 60 min monitoring. Flagged original port 12 for replacement and logged switch module failure.",resolved,160,123,22,IRC5 Network Switch Port12 CRC-errors ethernet_packet_loss_pct=6.50% action: moved to spare port & disabled EEE,irc5_communications_ethernet_packet_loss,fm_ethernet_network_has_problems,,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0014,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1014,2025-11-02T07:28:16Z,2025-11-02T07:31:16Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,drive_motor_overtemperature,alarm,CM,closed,18,ERROR,comp_drive_module,Drive module (left bank) — Axis 1 motor,1456,OVERTEMP_01,drive_motor_temp_c,87,C,75,C,WO-IRC5-1014,Axis 1 drive motor overtemp — front cabinet fan clogged,"During a production run the IRC5 reported drive_motor_temp_c at 87°C on axis 1 (threshold 75°C). Front intake fan (left bank) was heavily loaded with lint and filter media collapsed; fan RPM measured 1,200 rpm (spec ~3,000 rpm). Cabinet ambient 36°C and drive heatsink surface 64°C; operator reported reduced throughput before alarm. Unit cooled after shutdown and cleaning, no mechanical damage observed.","Powered down controller, waited until heatsink <45°C before working. Removed front panel and cleaned lint from axial fan and intake filter, replaced torn filter element, verified fan now spins 3,050 rpm and 24 V supply steady at 24.1 V. Performed two test cycles, monitored axis 1 temp falling to 58°C under same load, closed ticket.",resolved,119,93,23,IRC5 drive module axis1 fan clogged drive_motor_temp_c=87C cleaned/replaced filter RPM1200->3050,irc5_drive_motor_overtemperature,fm_components_overheated,,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0039,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1039,2025-11-18T15:03:28Z,2025-11-18T15:06:28Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,pm_brake_inspection,maintenance,PM,completed,10,INFO,comp_robot_brakes,Brake assemblies - mechanical linkages,,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1039,Mechanical linkage PM — pivot lubrication and torque check,Routine PM of brake mechanical linkages and mounting hardware. Found slight tick during engage on axis coupling but within tolerance; engage time measured 90 ms and holding torque satisfactory. Pivot pins cleaned and light film of grease applied to reduce noise. Mount bolts checked and torqued to spec.,"Disassembled linkage covers, cleaned pivot points, applied Molykote dry film lube to pivot and cam surfaces, torque-checked mounting bolts to 8.5 Nm, reassembled and re-tested engage cycle.",resolved,147,167,162,"IRC5, brake linkages, engage time 90ms, slight engage noise, pivot cleaned and lubricated, bolts torqued 8.5Nm",irc5_pm_brake_inspection,,,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0009,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1009,2025-11-25T17:34:22Z,2025-11-25T17:35:22Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,flexpendant_disconnected,alarm,CM,closed,14,WARN,comp_flexpendant,Pendant HMI cable / RJ45,,,flexpendant_connected,0,boolean,1,boolean,WO-IRC5-1009,"Comm error popup — pendant LED flicker during operation, cable kink found","During a long run operators saw a 'FlexPendant disconnected' comm popup and the pendant power LED flickered intermittently; IRC5 reported flexpendant_connected=0. Inspection revealed a pronounced kink and abrasion at the floor-entry point of the pendant cable causing intermittent contact. Replaced the inline patch with a known-good spare, re-routed cable away from the floor bend and verified stable connection.","1) Logged alarm and stopped cell. 2) Removed damaged patch section and installed spare shielded RJ45 patch cable, re-routed to eliminate kink. 3) Applied heat-shrink at strain relief and clamped cable to cabinet to prevent movement. 4) Confirmed continuity <1 Ω between ends and verified flexpendant_connected=1 for 20 min under motion test.",resolved,73,101,23,"IRC5 FlexPendant flicker, flexpendant_connected=0, cable kink/abrasion, replaced patch cable, reseat RJ45, continuity OK",irc5_flexpendant_disconnected,fm_cable_connector_damaged,sym_flexpendant_starts_but_does_not_communicate_with_controller,link_replaced_by_embedding,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0015,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1015,2025-11-27T16:46:48Z,2025-11-27T16:46:48Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,drive_motor_overtemperature,alarm,CM,closed,14,WARN,comp_drive_module,Drive module — Axis 2 (right bank),2320,,drive_motor_temp_c,80,C,75,C,WO-IRC5-1015,Axis 2 high temp — airflow blocked by material at cabinet side,"Drive motor temp for axis 2 reached 80°C during a morning shift while the cell HVAC was running nominally. Found cardboard boxes stacked flush against controller side vents, restricting exhaust airflow; cabinet ambient measured 42°C directly at vent. Heatsink temps were elevated (~66°C) but fan and wiring OK.",Removed obstructions and re-routed operator staging area to restore clear airflow path. Installed temporary ducting to direct exhaust away from work area and taped warning label on cabinet; monitored next 4 cycles — axis 2 stabilized around 70°C. Advised production to maintain 150 mm clearance.,resolved,119,88,92,IRC5 drive module axis2 blocked airflow drive_motor_temp_c=80C removed obstruction installed ducting,irc5_drive_motor_overtemperature,fm_components_overheated,sym_gearbox_axis_6_getting_hot,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0024,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1024,2025-11-29T09:02:01Z,2025-11-29T09:02:01Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,main_computer_event_log_errors,fault,CM,closed,14,WARN,comp_main_computer,Main computer (CPU board),,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1024,"IRC5 main computer — rising event log error count over 24h, cleared","Over a 24‑hour window the main computer event log showed a gradual ramp from ~4 errors/hr to peaks of ~20 errors/hr (total 128 entries). Errors were mixed: Ethernet comms CRCs on ETH0, intermittent I/O timeouts to I/O module A, and several generic system layer kernel warnings. CPU LED was observed amber on one occasion; main_computer_supply_vdc measured 23.8 VDC steady and rack temp 48°C. Logged findings and cleared event log after corrective actions; no repeat during 2‑hour post‑check.","Pulled full diagnostic package (syslog + eventlog) to USB; reseated ETH0 RJ45 and I/O backplane connector; reseated main computer daughterboard and tightened PSU terminal screws; soft reboot of controller (controlled STOP, restart via Service menu), cleared event log, monitored 2 hours with no recurrence.",resolved,98,185,34,"IRC5 main computer; event_log_error_count=128/24h; comms CRC, I/O timeout, cpu_led=amber, main_computer_supply_vdc=23.8V; reseated ETH0 and daughterboard, collected diagnostics",irc5_main_computer_event_log_error_count_high,fm_main_computer_has_problems,sym_dsqc_662_status_led_pulsing_green,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0058,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1058,2025-12-03T19:12:13Z,2025-12-03T19:15:13Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,bearing_wear_indication,condition_monitoring,PDM,open,14,WARN,comp_bearing,J2 motor bearing (axis 2),,,bearing_vibration_rms_mm_s,6.99,mm/s,4.0,mm/s,WO-IRC5-1058,"J2 bearing: rising vibration trend, recommend planned bearing replacement",PDM alarm flagged elevated RMS vibration on axis 2 (J2 motor bearing) at 6.99 mm/s (threshold 4.0). Trend review: weekly spectral logs show progressive rise from ~3.1 mm/s six weeks ago to current 6.99 mm/s; audible bearing chirp noted at low speed. Motor housing temp 48°C; visual inspection of motor flange found slight corrosion but no obvious grease leakage.,Isolated J2 and ran low-speed diagnostic cycle (100°/s) to confirm signature. Clamped accelerometer to motor housing and captured 1/3/5 kHz spectra (saved to PDM server). Tightened four motor flange bolts to 28 Nm (was loose by ~4 Nm). Logged replacement of bearing on next planned shutdown and raised work order for spare bearing (part ABB-REF J2-BEAR-001). Continued monitoring.,monitoring,115,141,19,IRC5 J2 motor bearing vibration_rms_mm_s=6.99 mm/s trending up; axis2; flange bolts retorqued; plan bearing replacement,irc5_bearings_wear_pdm,fm_bearings_damaged_or_worn,,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0010,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1010,2025-12-15T19:56:29Z,2025-12-15T19:59:29Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,flexpendant_disconnected,alarm,CM,closed,18,ERROR,comp_flexpendant,DSQC-662 / FlexPendant power feed,,,flexpendant_connected,0,boolean,1,boolean,WO-IRC5-1010,"Full pendant power loss — 0 V at DSQC-662, reconnected power feed","Pendant went completely dead with no LEDs illuminated and screen black; controller logged flexpendant_connected=0 and an ERROR-level disconnect. Technician measured 0 V at the DSQC-662 24 V input and found the DC jack had backed out from the power supply in the cabinet. Re-seated the DC connector, verified 24.3 V DC, powered pendant back on and confirmed normal operation.","1) Opened cabinet, isolated DC power. 2) Measured DSQC-662 input = 0 V; inspected DC jack and found loose seating. 3) Re-seated and secured DC plug on power supply, retightened connector mounting, measured 24.3 V DC. 4) Powered system, cleared alarms, performed pendant handshake and 10 min functional check.",resolved,66,114,8,"IRC5 FlexPendant dead, flexpendant_connected=0, 0V at DSQC-662 then 24.3V after reseat, reconnected DC feed, secured jack",irc5_flexpendant_disconnected,fm_flexpendant_power_supply_from_controller_faulty,sym_flexpendant_dead,link_replaced_by_embedding,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0061,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1061,2025-12-18T10:52:33Z,2025-12-18T10:56:33Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,operator_note_vibration,operator_note,,open,10,INFO,,"Arm assembly (elbow/wrist joints, Axis 2/4)",,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1061,Operator note: rough vibration on arm during high-speed moves,"Operator reports a rough, scratchy vibration coming from the arm during high-speed traverses (felt around elbow/wrist). Audible rattling only when motion >1.2 m/s, most noticeable on fast straight moves between stations; no status lights or error beeps on the IRC5. Quick visual check of gear covers and cabling found no loose bolts; motor driver temps normal (~38 °C). Logged the occurrence and advised ops — production continued.","noted, continued production",,,19,1,"IRC5 | Arm assembly (Axis 2/4) | rough vibration, audible rattle during >1.2 m/s moves | motor temp 38°C | no alarms | noted, continued production",irc5_operator_note_unknown_vibration,,sym_gearbox_axis_6_getting_hot,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0004,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1004,2025-12-20T13:22:13Z,2025-12-20T13:22:13Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,ethernet_packet_loss,alarm,CM,open,18,ERROR,comp_communications,Machine-area Ethernet run (near welder),,,ethernet_packet_loss_pct,8.7,%,2.0,%,WO-IRC5-1004,Interference from nearby welder causing link instability,Observed 8.7% packet loss concurrent with welding ops; timing correlated to 200 A pulse weld cycles. Ethernet cable routed parallel to welder return and ground strap — measured 60 mV AC noise on cable shield and saw link drops when welder fired. Controller link reported frequent carrier loss events (LINK DOWN flashes).,"Rerouted Ethernet away from welder ground conductors, applied ferrite clamp to cable at the controller end and installed additional ground strap on welder; set switch port to 100Base-TX (forced) to reduce auto-negotiation noise. Monitoring in open state — improvement noted but continued scheduled weld tests required.",monitoring,111,98,71,IRC5 Communications EMI welder-noise ethernet_packet_loss_pct=8.70% action: rerouted cable + ferrite clamp; controller link dropped during weld pulses,irc5_communications_ethernet_packet_loss,fm_ethernet_network_has_problems,,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0044,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1044,2025-12-21T17:52:35Z,2025-12-21T17:55:35Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,pm_gearbox_oil_change,maintenance,PM,completed,10,INFO,comp_gearboxes,Axis 3 reducer (GBX-3),,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1044,PM: Axis 3 gearbox oil change — slight discoloration only,"Completed preventive oil change on IRC5 Axis 3 gearbox. Drained 160 mL of Mobil SHC 629 220; oil was slightly darker than new but no metallic flakes seen on the drain magnet. Checked filler O‑ring which showed minor surface wear; no active seepage. Refilled, tightened plugs, and performed a 5‑minute low-speed run — vibration and torque within normal limits.","Shutdown and locked out power, drained oil into collection bottle (sample tagged A3-20251221), replaced filler O‑ring with spare (PN O-RING-3), cleaned and reinstalled drain plug (torque 13 Nm). Refilled to spec with Mobil SHC 629 220, bled gearbox, executed run-in procedure and updated CMMS with observations.",resolved,212,221,186,IRC5 gearbox axis3 MobilSHC629220 drained_qty=160mL discoloration=slight O-ring=replaced run_test_ok,irc5_pm_gearbox_oil_change,,sym_oil_and_grease_stains_on_motors_and_gearboxes,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0016,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1016,2025-12-26T11:19:50Z,2025-12-26T11:21:50Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,drive_motor_overtemperature,alarm,CM,open,18,ERROR,comp_drive_module,Drive module (axis6 gearbox),5012,HOT_AX6,drive_motor_temp_c,91,C,75,C,WO-IRC5-1016,Axis 6 overtemp with high ambient and new program duty ramp,"Axis 6 drive_motor_temp_c peaked at 91°C during a new fast-cycle program; threshold 75°C. Ambient in robot cell was elevated (measured 46°C) due to nearby process heaters and the new PLC recipe increased axis 6 duty cycle by ~30%, causing continuous high torque periods. Gearbox surface measured 78°C and bleeder_temp_c trending high; no fan mechanical failure found.","Placed cell into restricted production, reduced axis 6 speed/accel in program to lower duty for immediate cooling, enabled auxiliary cabinet fan on high manually. Logged temperature trend and left unit under monitoring — will schedule thermal performance test with process engineering and consider adding conditioned air or a dedicated cooling unit. Ticket left open for follow-up.",monitoring,130,123,105,IRC5 drive module axis6 high ambient program duty drive_motor_temp_c=91C reduced speed enabled aux fan monitoring,irc5_drive_motor_overtemperature,fm_components_overheated,sym_gearbox_axis_6_getting_hot,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0062,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1062,2025-12-28T12:10:51Z,2025-12-28T12:13:51Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,operator_note_vibration,operator_note,,open,10,INFO,,IRC5 cabinet cooling fan / baseplate,,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1062,Idle cabinet humming felt at base — operator note,"Operator heard a low-frequency humming coming from the IRC5 cabinet while robot was idle; vibration transmitted through baseplate. Cabinet fan audible, vibration stops when fan speed drops; cabinet skin temp near fan measured ~45 °C and fan speed estimated ~2.8k RPM. No IR or system alarm messages present and LEDs were normal (green). Event logged; operator resumed production.","noted, continued production",,,25,2,"IRC5 | cabinet cooling fan / baseplate | low-frequency hum at idle, cabinet temp 45°C, fan ~2800 RPM | no alarms | noted, continued production",irc5_operator_note_unknown_vibration,,,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0031,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1031,2026-01-05T12:45:01Z,2026-01-05T12:46:01Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,tcp_calibration_drift,condition_monitoring,PDM,closed,14,WARN,comp_robot_arm_system,robot arm system (A1-A6) - tool flange,1047,,tcp_position_drift_mm,2.09,mm,0.5,mm,WO-IRC5-1031,IRC5 TCP drift 2.09 mm after minor collision — four-point recalibration,"During weekly check TCP position drift measured 2.09 mm (threshold 0.5 mm). Operator reported a light bump into a parts fixture the previous shift; small scrape found on tool flange bolt head and encoder cover on A2. Drive DC bus 560 V, joint temps A2/A3 48°C/52°C, servo currents nominal; revolution counters on A2 were +0.8 rev vs stored position. Performed four-point + fine TCP calibration and verified repeatability at tool flange to 0.15 mm.","Powered down cell, inspected tool flange and re-torqued 4x M6 bolts (10 Nm), cleared collision log, reset encoder zero on A2, updated revolution counters, executed four-point calibration then fine calibration routine; validated repeatability with 10-point verification (max error 0.15 mm).",resolved,130,132,111,IRC5 robot arm system tool flange TCP tcp_position_drift_mm=2.09mm collision bump revcounters updated ERR1047 action:four-point+fine_cal,irc5_robot_tcp_calibration_drift,fm_robot_not_calibrated_correctly,sym_inconsistent_path_accuracy,link_replaced_by_embedding,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0028,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1028,2026-01-05T18:01:07Z,2026-01-05T18:02:07Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,dsqc_662_fault,fault,CM,closed,18,ERROR,comp_dsqc_662,DSQC 662 PSU,662,DSQC_662_FAULT,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1028,DSQC 662 comm fault — reseated X1/X2 and reflowed suspect joint,Controller logged DSQC_662 communication fault during an Axis 3 move; robot stopped with intermittent comm alarms. Visual inspection inside cabinet found X1 shield not fully latched and slight axial play on X2; DSQC 662 PCB showed a cold solder appearance at the U5 regulator corner and component temp ~48°C. After reseating connectors the error cleared and no further trips in verification run.,"Powered down controller, removed lower door, inspected X1/X2 connectors and cable screens; cleaned pins with contact cleaner, reseated and latched X1/X2, tightened connector screws to ~0.6 Nm. Reflowed suspected dry solder joint at U5 with temperature-controlled iron, bench-tested three full motion cycles of Axis 1–6, logged event and closed ticket.",resolved,101,156,151,IRC5 DSQC_662 X1/X2 connectors dry_solder joint joint_contact_resistance_ohm=0.12Ω connector_torque_nm=0.6 ACTION=reseated+reflow,irc5_dsqc_662_fault,FM-076,SYM-025,link_replaced_by_embedding,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01"", ""error_code_source"": ""controller_event_log""}"
log_irc5_0046,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1046,2026-01-10T15:12:01Z,2026-01-10T15:13:01Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,usb_communication_error,alarm,CM,closed,14,WARN,comp_usb_connection,DSQC 662 USB A (rear PSU connector),,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1046,USB flash drive failed to enumerate; backup restored with known-good drive,"During a scheduled backup the DSQC 662 status LED was pulsing green and the USB device did not enumerate on the IRC5 controller (no VID/PID visible on laptop). Vbus measured 5.05 V at the connector but the original SanDisk 32GB stick showed no response on two different cables. Replaced with a known-good SanDisk Cruzer 16GB (FAT32), LED returned to steady green and backup completed successfully within 12 minutes.","Inspected USB A cable and connectors; continuity check on cable OK. Tried original drive in laptop (failed), swapped to known-good 16GB drive, formatted FAT32, soft-rebooted IRC5 controller (controller reboot ~90 s). Verified dsqc662_status_led steady green and confirmed backup file written to new drive. Logged serial number of failed drive and placed in parts bin for quarantine.",resolved,53,50,13,IRC5 DSQC662 USB_connection dsqc662_status_led=pulsing_green usb_connected=disconnected VBUS=5.05V action=swapped_drive backup_completed,irc5_usb_communication_error,fm_usb_communication_error,sym_dsqc_662_status_led_pulsing_green,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0059,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1059,2026-01-13T13:10:48Z,2026-01-13T13:11:48Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,bearing_wear_indication,condition_monitoring,PDM,open,10,INFO,comp_bearing,J3 gearbox bearing (axis 3),,,bearing_vibration_rms_mm_s,8.89,mm/s,4.0,mm/s,WO-IRC5-1059,Axis 3 gearbox bearing high RMS (8.89 mm/s) — monitoring only,PDM detected a high vibration peak on axis 3 gearbox bearing with RMS 8.89 mm/s (threshold 4.0). Operator reported intermittent mechanical noise during high-accel moves; motor housing temp measured 62°C at end of shift. No visible oil contamination on gearbox breather; coupling alignment within tolerance but slight axial play felt at gearbox input.,Noted condition and kept axis in service under reduced duty cycle; escalated to maintenance planner for scheduled gearbox/bearing inspection on next maintenance window. Collected additional run-up vibration file and attached to work order (file: VIB_J3_20260113.WAV).,monitoring,109,85,54,"IRC5 J3 gearbox bearing vibration_rms_mm_s=8.89 mm/s; axis3; elevated temp 62C; logged sample, reduced duty, schedule inspection",irc5_bearings_wear_pdm,fm_bearings_damaged_or_worn,sym_gearbox_axis_6_getting_hot,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0021,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1021,2026-01-15T14:36:50Z,2026-01-15T14:40:50Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,brake_release_fault,fault,CM,closed,18,ERROR,comp_robot_brakes,Brakes Axis 4 - coil and harness,,,brake_release_voltage_vdc,20.23,VDC,24,VDC,WO-IRC5-1021,Axis 4 brake coil worn; harness damage found and coil replaced,"Robot failed to release Axis 4 brake on motion with brake_release_voltage_vdc = 20.23 VDC (spec 24 VDC). Secured arm with a mechanical stop and verified continuity was intermittent at a pinch point in the harness; measured brake coil resistance high at 110.6 Ω indicating partial open/worn coil. Removed Axis 4 brake assembly, replaced brake coil and repaired the damaged harness section, then tested — coil resistance stabilized at 46.2 Ω and release voltage restored to 24.6 VDC. Performed motion validation and returned the cell to production.","Secured arm with mechanical stop; powered down servos; measured VDC and coil R (20.23 V, 110.6 Ω); inspected harness, found chafed insulation and intermittent continuity; cut out damaged section and installed new shielded lead, replaced brake coil assembly on Axis 4, re-terminated connectors, powered up and verified brake_release_voltage = 24.6 V and normal brake operation; logged parts used and test report.",resolved,167,292,118,IRC5 Axis 4 brakes; brake_release_voltage_vdc=20.23V; coil_res=110.6Ω (worn); harness damaged; replaced coil and harness; verified 24.6V,irc5_brake_release_fault,fm_holding_brakes_disconnected_faulty_worn_or_non_operational,sym_holding_brakes_non_operational,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0032,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1032,2026-01-17T21:01:02Z,2026-01-17T21:04:02Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,tcp_calibration_drift,condition_monitoring,PDM,closed,14,WARN,comp_robot_arm_system,end effector - pneumatic gripper (tool flange),,,tcp_position_drift_mm,0.81,mm,0.5,mm,WO-IRC5-1032,Gripper change caused TCP offset 0.81 mm — tool re-cal and verify,"Routine weekly calibration flagged TCP drift of 0.81 mm. Noted recent end-effector swap: pneumatic gripper replaced and new alignment shim fitted two days earlier. Tool data showed incorrect mass value (read 2.0 kg vs actual 2.35 kg) and tool length off by 4 mm, causing measured offset; joint A6 encoder temperature 38°C, DB bus steady at 540 V.","Removed gripper, re-measured tool center point with four-point method, updated tool mass/offset in IRC5 tool data, ran fine calibration to correct orientation, re-mounted gripper and torqued mounting studs to spec; final verification repeatability 0.10 mm.",resolved,136,203,60,IRC5 end effector (gripper) TCP tcp_position_drift_mm=0.81mm tool_change updated_tooldata action:fine_cal,irc5_robot_tcp_calibration_drift,fm_robot_not_calibrated_correctly,sym_inconsistent_path_accuracy,link_replaced_by_embedding,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0065,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1065,2026-01-19T21:45:45Z,2026-01-19T21:49:45Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,gearbox_oil_temperature_high,alarm,CM,closed,18,ERROR,comp_gearboxes,GBX Axis 4 (output stage),,,gearbox_oil_temp_c,89,C,85,C,WO-IRC5-1065,"Axis 4 gearbox oil overtemp — 89°C, reduced duty cycle","During a sustained high-duty welding run the Axis 4 gearbox oil temp climbed to 89°C (threshold 85°C) for ~135 minutes. Ambient in cell measured ~36°C and gearbox housing surface ~68°C; no external oil leaks observed. Sight-glass showed oil level within min-max and oil appeared dark but not frothy. Logged alarm, reduced duty cycle to 50%, improved cell ventilation, and monitored until temperature returned to normal.",Put robot into MAINT hold and reduced program duty cycle; opened cell vent and started additional portable extraction fan; verified sight-glass oil level and compared to manual recommended level/type; circulated gearbox by jogging axes at low speed for 10 min; recorded temps every 10 min until stable; returned to production with reduced duty for remainder of shift.,resolved,177,135,100,IRC5 GBX Axis4 gearbox_oil_temp_c=89C threshold85C duty_cycle_reduced oil_level_checked ambient36C ventilation_started,irc5_gearbox_oil_temperature_high,fm_gearbox_oil_too_hot,sym_gearbox_axis_6_getting_hot,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0045,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1045,2026-01-24T15:18:28Z,2026-01-24T15:21:28Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,pm_gearbox_oil_change,maintenance,PM,completed,10,INFO,comp_gearboxes,GBX6 (axis 6 gearbox),,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1045,"PM: Axis 6 gearbox oil change — metal flakes present, sample sent",Performed scheduled oil change on IRC5 Axis 6 gearbox. Drained 140 mL Mobilgear 600 XP 220; magnetic drain plug collected dark metallic flakes (~0.5 g) and oil had slight grey tinge. No obvious external leaks or overheating observed (housing temp 50°C at drain). Logged condition as 'requires engineering review' and sent oil plus debris for metallurgical analysis.,"Isolated controller, drained gearbox and removed magnetic plug, scraped and weighed debris (0.5 g) and placed in lab container (sample ID LAB-A6-20260124). Cleaned magnet, reinstalled drain plug (torqued 15 Nm), refilled with Mobilgear 600 XP 220 to level, cycled axis through full range at reduced speed, captured vibration and current traces, and uploaded results to CMMS for follow-up.",resolved,232,205,182,IRC5 gearbox axis6 Mobilgear600XP220 drained_qty=140mL metal_particles=0.5g temp=50C sample_sent; vibration logged,irc5_pm_gearbox_oil_change,,sym_gearbox_axis_6_getting_hot,date_imputed,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0049,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1049,2026-01-27T07:52:34Z,2026-01-27T07:52:34Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,mains_power_loss,fault,CM,closed,22,FATAL,comp_controller,IRC5 controller mains input (PDU / main power terminal),1001,MAINS_LOSS,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1049,"Facility mains outage — IRC5 lost incoming supply, no mains present","During morning shift the IRC5 went down with no mains input. Main switch was in the ON position but measured mains at L1-L3 = 0 VAC at the controller mains strip; building electrical reported a facility-wide outage. F2 fuse found intact, F6 breaker not tripped, and transformer T1 primary and secondary measured 0 VAC while outage persisted. After building power was restored I re-checked T1 (primary 400 VAC, secondary 24 VAC), confirmed service outlet still dead until building crew energised distribution, then brought controller back online and ran full diagnostics.","Applied LOTO, confirmed building electrical outage with facilities; measured incoming mains at controller L1-L3 = 0 VAC; inspected main switch (ON), verified F2 fuse continuity (OK) and F6 breaker position (ON/not tripped); monitored transformer T1 (0 VAC during outage); when site power restored measured mains 400 VAC L-L and T1 secondary 24 VAC; powered controller, cleared alarms, verified HMI boot and axis enable. Restored USB comm to power-supply laptop and logged event.",resolved,105,219,160,"IRC5 controller mains input, facility outage, mains_voltage_vac 0 VAC, main switch ON, F2 intact, F6 ON, T1 0V during outage -> restored to 400VAC/24VAC, cleared alarms, diagnostics",irc5_mains_power_supply_loss,fm_mains_power_supply_loss,sym_no_voltage_in_service_outlet,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0025,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1025,2026-01-29T11:34:30Z,2026-01-29T11:36:30Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,main_computer_event_log_errors,fault,CM,open,18,ERROR,comp_main_computer,Main computer - CPU unit (front rack),21045,FW_POST_UPDATE,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1025,IRC5 main computer — sudden overnight spike after firmware activity,"Event log shows a sudden spike (412 entries in 24h) starting 01:12 local time with concentrated FlexPendant timeouts, SERCOS I/O disconnects and repeated system watchdog events. Spike began 8 hours after a controller firmware patch was applied; CPU temperature measured at 72°C during the spike and supply dipped to 21.6 VDC briefly at 01:15. Controller experienced two automatic soft reboots (no full power loss) and FlexPendant dropped comms several times; issue remains active.","Exported full diagnostics (DiagPack + event snapshots) to support USB and opened ticket with ABB TAC; rolled back non‑critical network changes, isolated pendant network (moved to spare switch port), left controller running under supervision, disabled non‑essential I/O modules for stability while monitoring. Awaiting TAC instruction for firmware rollback or hotfix.",monitoring,86,93,56,"IRC5 main computer; event_log_error_count=412/24h; FlexPendant timeouts, SERCOS disconnects, main_computer_supply_vdc=21.6V dip, cpu_temp=72C; collected diag, isolated pendant network",irc5_main_computer_event_log_error_count_high,fm_flexpendant_power_supply_from_controller_faulty,sym_erratic_event_messages_on_flexpendant,link_replaced_by_embedding,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0005,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1005,2026-01-30T08:42:35Z,2026-01-30T08:45:35Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,ethernet_packet_loss,alarm,CM,closed,14,WARN,comp_communications,AXC I/O Comm Module (I/O rack),3002,,ethernet_packet_loss_pct,11.48,%,2.0,%,WO-IRC5-1005,"AXC module LED stuck OFF — I/O comms degraded, high packet loss",Measured 11.48% packet loss concomitant with AXC I/O instability. Found AXC module LED (top of I/O rack) stuck OFF while controller link to AXC showed DOWN; physical inspection revealed slightly dislodged AXC module and one bent pins on backplane connector. FlexPendant would start but then lose image during the fault.,"Powered down controller rack, reseated AXC module and cleaned backplane contacts with IPA, replaced AXC with spare module (swap) to confirm fault. After swap, AXC LED green and controller link UP, packet loss returned to 0.2% on 30-min test. Original AXC sent for bench test and repair.",resolved,146,69,67,IRC5 AXC I/O module AXC_LED_OFF ethernet_packet_loss_pct=11.48% action: reseated & swapped AXC module; controller link restored,irc5_communications_ethernet_packet_loss,fm_ethernet_network_has_problems,SYM-023,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0017,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1017,2026-02-05T21:42:03Z,2026-02-05T21:44:03Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,drive_motor_overtemperature,alarm,CM,closed,18,ERROR,comp_drive_module,Drive module fan controller (right bank),3209,FAN_PWM_LOW,drive_motor_temp_c,81,C,75,C,WO-IRC5-1017,Fan controller PWM low — drives running hot (axis bank),"Drive motor temp rose to 81°C across the right drive bank with prolonged runtime; alarm reported fan duty low. Inspection showed fan PWM controller output at 35% duty (expected >80% above 60°C); one MOSFET on fan driver PCB had intermittent solder joints and low gate drive. Cabinet ambient 38°C, DC bus 535 Vdc within nominal range.","Shutdown and isolated power, reflowed suspect MOSFET joints and replaced corrupted gate driver IC. Reprogrammed fan curve in IRC5 parameter set to ensure 100% at 60°C+. Brought system up, confirmed fans reach 3,200 rpm and temps stabilize at 64–68°C under load. Closed after 24-hour verification run.",resolved,175,196,107,IRC5 drive module fan controller PWM low drive_motor_temp_c=81C replaced gate driver reprogrammed fan curve,irc5_drive_motor_overtemperature,fm_components_overheated,,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0040,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1040,2026-02-09T19:58:38Z,2026-02-09T20:02:38Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,pm_brake_inspection,maintenance,PM,completed,10,INFO,comp_robot_brakes,Axis 5 holding brake,,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1040,"PM brake inspection — Axis 5 lining below threshold, restricted ops set","PM check on Axis 5 found lining thickness 1.3 mm (below service threshold 1.5 mm) and the brake showed slight slip during dynamic torque test. No contamination present, wear appears end-of-life. Placed axis in restricted mode pending replacement and raised priority for brake kit procurement.","Measured lining (1.3 mm), performed dynamic torque verification (observed slip at rated torque), set axis to restricted speed/torque in controller, attached red 'do not use' tag, raised part order and scheduled corrective PM.",resolved,168,199,150,"IRC5, Axis 5 brake, lining 1.3mm (below limit), slip on torque test, axis restricted and replacement ordered",irc5_pm_brake_inspection,,sym_holding_brakes_non_operational,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0011,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1011,2026-02-09T21:36:04Z,2026-02-09T21:36:04Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,flexpendant_disconnected,alarm,CM,closed,14,WARN,comp_flexpendant,FlexPendant cable (field routing),,,flexpendant_connected,0,boolean,1,boolean,WO-IRC5-1011,Intermittent disconnects during heavy motion; cable clamped against motor conduit,"Pendant would drop out for 2–5 seconds intermittently during high-speed moves on axis 2 and 4, with power LED remaining green but flexpendant_connected toggling to 0. Visual check showed pendant cable routed tightly against AC motor conduit and a cable clamp compressing the outer braid causing intermittent grounding/shielding faults; ambient cabinet temp measured 44°C. Rerouted cable away from conduit, replaced clamp with vibration-tolerant clamp and added a ferrite bead; connection stabilized.","1) Reproduced disconnect by running axis 2/4 routine; monitored pendant connection. 2) Loosened and repositioned cable away from motor conduit, removed sharp clamp, installed vibration clamp and ferrite. 3) Reseated RJ45 at both ends, verified flexpendant_connected=1. 4) Ran 30 min motion cycle; no further drops observed.",resolved,52,80,6,"IRC5 FlexPendant intermittent disconnect, flexpendant_connected=0 during axis motion, cable clamped vs conduit, reroute and add ferrite, reseat RJ45",irc5_flexpendant_disconnected,fm_flexpendant_cable_damaged,sym_flexpendant_starts_but_does_not_communicate_with_controller,link_replaced_by_embedding,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0066,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1066,2026-02-10T14:44:34Z,2026-02-10T14:44:34Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,gearbox_oil_temperature_high,alarm,CM,closed,14,WARN,comp_gearboxes,"gearbox Axis 2 (GB2, input side)",,,gearbox_oil_temp_c,89,C,85,C,WO-IRC5-1066,"Axis 2 gearbox temp warning — sustained 89°C, top-up and breather clean","Axis 2 reported gearbox oil at 89°C for ~215 minutes while running a continuous high-load pick-and-place cycle; ambient thermistor read 42°C contributing to heat soak. Inspection found oil level ~5 mm below lower sight-glass mark and breather vent partially clogged with grime. Topped up to spec oil level, cleaned/replaced breather cap, and cycled the axis to re-establish normal oil circulation.",Stopped program and set axis to slow jog; cleaned gearbox breather and replaced silicone vent element; topped gearbox to recommended oil level/type per manual; logged serial and batch of oil used; monitored gearbox_oil_temp_c for 3 hrs — temp trended downward to within limits; cleared warning and resumed production with duty-cycle advisory.,resolved,156,215,66,IRC5 gearboxes Axis2 gearbox_oil_temp_c=89C ambient42C oil_level_low topped_up breather_cleaned duty_cycle_advisory,irc5_gearbox_oil_temperature_high,fm_gearbox_oil_too_hot,sym_gearbox_axis_6_getting_hot,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0051,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1051,2026-02-10T19:22:44Z,2026-02-10T19:23:44Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,joystick_anomaly,fault,CM,closed,14,WARN,comp_joystick,"FlexPendant joystick (FP-JOY, ribbon J2)",,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1051,FlexPendant joystick shows constant X deflection while idle,"Operator reported a steady X-axis deflection on the FlexPendant while nobody was touching the stick. Pendant diagnostics showed X at -8% on the jog display and ADC X-center = 0.42 V (expected ≈0.50 V); 5 V supply to joystick PCB confirmed. Physical inspection found the ribbon connector (J2) on the joystick PCB slightly loosened and green corrosion on one pad, causing a persistent offset.","Set controller to manual, powered down pendant and removed back cover. Cleaned contact corrosion with isopropyl and contact cleaner, reseated J2 ribbon, torqued connector latch, recalibrated joystick center in IRC5 service menu. Verified idle within ±1% and performed jog test on J1/J2; returned to service.",resolved,84,66,35,IRC5 FlexPendant joystick X-axis offset -8% ADC X=0.42V 5V OK reseated J2 cleaned contact recalibrated,irc5_joystick_anomaly,fm_joystick_malfunctioning,,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0047,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1047,2026-02-11T15:46:44Z,2026-02-11T15:48:44Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,usb_communication_error,alarm,CM,closed,14,WARN,comp_usb_connection,"USB port on DSQC 662 (left bank, rear panel)",,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1047,Intermittent USB port contact; reflowed connector and moved to alternate port,Operator reported repeated USB disconnects during restore; DSQC 662 LED pulsed green intermittently. Continuity check on the installed cable showed intermittent open at the A‑shell; swapping to the front USB port immediately stabilized the link. Visual inspection of the rear DSQC 662 connector revealed a cracked solder pad on the USB A shell.,"Removed cabinet cover, secured chassis to ground, desoldered and reflowed USB A connector on DSQC 662 left-bank port, cleaned flux residue with isopropyl, retested continuity (0.2 Ω on shell to board). Temporarily moved production backups to front port while monitoring; replaced user cable with new shielded USB A‑A cable and confirmed stable USB enumeration and backup completion. Recorded downtime and replaced defective cable permanently.",resolved,46,44,27,IRC5 DSQC662 USB_port pulsing_green dsqc662_status_led continuity=intermittent action=reflowed_connector swapped_port usb_connected=restored,irc5_usb_communication_error,fm_usb_communication_error,sym_dsqc_662_status_led_pulsing_green,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0063,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1063,2026-02-14T08:04:51Z,2026-02-14T08:08:51Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,operator_note_vibration,operator_note,,open,10,INFO,,Shoulder joint (Axis 3) gearbox / coupling,,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1063,Intermittent knocking on shoulder during acceleration — placed under monitoring,Operator observed a distinct knocking / impact noise from the shoulder (Axis 3) during rapid starts; knocks occur intermittently on acceleration profiles. Occurrence correlates to accel rates above ~1.5 rad/s² and is not present at low-speed jogging; no drive faults or LED warnings on the IRC5. Technician enabled logging of motion trace and vibration channel for the joint and instructed ops to temporarily reduce acceleration until further review. Status set to monitoring for trend capture.,"placed under monitoring; motion/vibration trace enabled, ops asked to reduce accel",monitoring,,21,7,"IRC5 | Shoulder joint (Axis 3) | intermittent knock on accel >1.5 rad/s², no drive faults, vibration trace enabled | monitoring",irc5_operator_note_unknown_vibration,,,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0055,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1055,2026-02-14T11:47:45Z,2026-02-14T11:50:45Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,flexpendant_cable_damaged,fault,CM,closed,18,ERROR,comp_flexpendant_cable,FlexPendant cable (pinched near cell door),,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1055,"FlexPendant cable pinched in cell door; outer jacket crushed, replaced","Operator reported pendant dead after closing cell door. Visual inspection found the jacket pinched and crushed ~150 mm from the connector where the cable had been trapped in the cell door hinge; braided shield visible and one conductor continuity failed. Performed LOTO, removed pendant, confirmed 24 V supply open under load (Fluke 87: OL on 24 V conductor), replaced cable assembly from spare (P/N FP-CBL-001). Re-routed cable through new strain relief, secured clamp at door entry, validated pendant powered (24.1 V) and full teach functions restored.","1) Lockout/tagout IRC5 and isolate power. 2) Remove FlexPendant and inspect cable; continuity check showed open on 24 V conductor. 3) Installed replacement FlexPendant cable (P/N FP-CBL-001) from onsite stock, tightened connector screws to 1.2 Nm, fitted new rubber strain relief and cable clamp. 4) Measured 24.1 V at pendant connector, exercised pendant jog and I/O, returned cell to production.",resolved,91,67,44,IRC5 FlexPendant cable pinched in door; jacket crushed near connector; 24V conductor open; replaced cable P/N FP-CBL-001; strain relief fitted,irc5_flexpendant_cable_damaged,fm_flexpendant_cable_damaged,sym_flexpendant_starts_but_does_not_communicate_with_controller,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0052,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1052,2026-02-19T07:44:58Z,2026-02-19T07:48:58Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,joystick_anomaly,fault,CM,closed,14,WARN,comp_joystick,FlexPendant joystick assembly (pot + PCB),,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1052,Large dead zone on pendant joystick — small moves not registered,"Operators observed that small joystick movements produced no motion until large deflection (~15%) was applied. Bench measurement of the potentiometer trace showed non-linear response and reduced slope across the first 15% of travel, indicating worn carbon track on the pot. Movement was repeatable on both X and Y axes when tested at 10% increments.","Set controller to manual and isolated pendant. Replaced joystick assembly (joystick pot + PCB) with shop spare, secured cable strain relief, and performed full calibration. Functional verification: linear response across 0–100% travel and smooth jog on J1, J2, J3. Logged old assembly for RMA.",resolved,76,109,50,IRC5 FlexPendant joystick dead-zone 15% worn pot replaced joystick assembly calibrated J1-J3,irc5_joystick_anomaly,fm_joystick_malfunctioning,sym_flexpendant_dead,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0068,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1068,2026-02-19T08:07:22Z,2026-02-19T08:07:22Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,service_tools_left_behind,fault,CM,closed,14,WARN,comp_robot_arm_system,IRC5 robot arm (base / Axis 1),,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1068,Torque wrench left at base — safety check stopped IRC5,"Post-PM robot executed safety check and went into protective stop when moving toward home. Found a 17mm torque wrench wedged between the robot base plate and cable guide under Axis 1, contacting the lower wrist cable bundle and triggering a collision/safety-check fault. No damage to arm or cables observed; brakes and gears felt normal when jogged slowly after removal.","Removed torque wrench from base, verified no visible cable abrasion, cycled IRC5 power, cleared protective stop, jogged axes 1–6 at reduced speed through full home sequence, re-ran safety check and brought unit back into manual mode. Updated PM checklist to add mandatory tool sweep and emailed maintenance lead with photo of location.",resolved,24,41,10,"IRC5; robot arm (base Axis 1); torque wrench left-behind; safety/protective stop; collision trigger; removed tool, cycled power, re-run safety check; PM checklist updated",irc5_service_tools_left_behind,fm_left_behind_service_tools,sym_holding_brakes_non_operational,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0022,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1022,2026-02-20T11:27:02Z,2026-02-20T11:29:02Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,brake_release_fault,fault,CM,closed,14,WARN,comp_robot_brakes,Axis 1 brake release relay (I/O board K4),,,brake_release_voltage_vdc,21.67,VDC,24,VDC,WO-IRC5-1022,Axis 1 release relay sticking; relay swapped and voltage restored,Intermittent brake release on Axis 1 observed; measured brake_release_voltage_vdc = 21.67 VDC (below 24 VDC threshold). Secured arm with a safety bar and checked coil resistance at 52.1 Ω (within spec) while finding the brake release relay on the I/O relay board exhibiting contact bounce and visual pitting. Swapped the suspect relay with a spare relay module and re-routed wiring to avoid chafe; after swap release voltage measured 24.2 VDC and several cycle tests showed repeatable brake release. Closed out after functional verification.,"Installed mechanical safety bar; isolated power to controller I/O; measured coil R = 52.1 Ω and Vdc = 21.67; inspected relay K4 on I/O board (pitting and sticking); removed and replaced relay with spare, secured connector, cycled power and measured brake_release_voltage = 24.2 V; completed 5-cycle motion test and documented replacement on CM log.",resolved,190,164,78,IRC5 Axis 1 brake; brake_release_voltage_vdc=21.67V; coil_res=52.1Ω; relay K4 sticking/replaced; relay swap restored 24.2V,irc5_brake_release_fault,fm_holding_brakes_disconnected_faulty_worn_or_non_operational,sym_holding_brakes_non_operational,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0026,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1026,2026-02-26T12:17:01Z,2026-02-26T12:18:01Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,main_computer_event_log_errors,fault,CM,closed,14,WARN,comp_main_computer,Main computer — controller cabinet (rear),,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1026,IRC5 main computer — single-night spike correlated with HVAC failure,"A single-night cluster of 56 event log errors occurred between 02:00–03:30, dominated by I/O RESP_TIMEOUT and a few ethernet comms resets. Local HVAC compressor fault recorded in building logs coincided with elevated cabinet temp (measured 68°C at controller rear) and CPU LED changed to amber briefly. After ambient temperature returned to normal the error rate dropped to baseline and no persistent faults on axes or IO cards were observed.","Copied event log and temperature snapshots to USB, power‑cycled controller once after HVAC restored, verified main_computer_supply_vdc = 24.1 VDC and CPU LED returned to green. Secured additional thermal logging and placed note for facilities to repair HVAC; closed while monitoring normal operation for next 24 hours.",resolved,80,58,23,"IRC5 main computer; event_log_error_count=56/24h single-night spike; I/O RESP_TIMEOUT, ethernet reset, rack_temp=68C; power-cycled after HVAC restore, collected logs",irc5_main_computer_event_log_error_count_high,fm_main_computer_has_problems,,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0056,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1056,2026-02-27T07:19:05Z,2026-02-27T07:22:05Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,flexpendant_cable_damaged,fault,CM,closed,18,ERROR,comp_flexpendant_cable,Pendant cable (mid-span abrasion),,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1056,Mid-cable abrasion exposing conductors — temporary repair and re-route,"Maintenance discovered a 60 mm long abrasion mid-span approximately 1.2 m from the pendant that had worn through the jacket and exposed inner conductors from chafing against a welded bracket. Continuity and resistance checks on all lines passed (24 V stable at 24.0 V, CAN pair continuity OK), so a temporary field repair was done to restore mechanical protection. Repaired with UV-resistant self-fusing silicone wrap, 3:1 heat-shrink reinforcement, and spiral wrap; cable re-routed away from bracket and clamped with a P-clamp.","1) De-energized pendant and performed insulation/continuity checks with Fluke DMM; all conductors passed. 2) Cleaned area, applied insulating mastic, then 3:1 heat shrink and spiral protective wrap over the exposed section; secured with stainless P-clamp to mounting rail. 3) Re-routed cable path to remove contact with bracket and logged service action with recommendation to replace cable at next scheduled downtime.",resolved,64,177,117,IRC5 FlexPendant cable mid-cable abrasion exposing conductors; continuity OK; 24.0V measured; temporary heat-shrink and spiral-wrap repair; re-routed and clamped,irc5_flexpendant_cable_damaged,fm_flexpendant_cable_damaged,,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0070,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1070,2026-02-27T13:26:57Z,2026-02-27T13:30:57Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,io_update_interval_too_low,alarm,CM,closed,14,WARN,comp_io_units,I/O rack 2 (new EtherNet/IP unit U4),,,io_update_interval_ms,34,ms,20,ms,WO-IRC5-1070,I/O update interval exceeded after new I/O unit added — io_update_interval_ms=34ms,"After commissioning a newly installed I/O unit on the EtherNet/IP rack, the IRC5 reported io_update_interval_ms = 34 ms (configured threshold 20 ms). Fieldbus throughput spikes observed and robot program showed intermittent sluggishness; io_bus_load measured at ~82% during the event. Reviewed RobotStudio I/O configuration and found the new unit set to continuous cyclic read for many channels which increased aggregate update traffic.","Inspected I/O rack 2 (new unit labeled U4) with RobotStudio; changed non-critical digital channels on U4 from cyclic to change-of-state where permissible, increased update interval for peripheral DI/DO groups from 20ms→100ms, restarted I/O task on IRC5 and verified io_bus_load fell to ~46%. Logged changes to maintenance record and left monitoring enabled for next 8 hours; production downtime ~19 min while restarting I/O task.",resolved,119,197,19,IRC5 I/O rack 2 io_update_interval_ms=34ms fieldbus_load=82% RobotStudio changed channels to COS and increased intervals,irc5_io_update_interval_too_low,fm_io_update_interval_too_low,,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0029,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1029,2026-02-27T13:43:37Z,2026-02-27T13:46:37Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,dsqc_662_fault,fault,CM,closed,18,ERROR,comp_dsqc_662,DSQC-662 card (power module),662,DSQC_662_COMM,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1029,Repeated DSQC 662 comm errors — replaced board after loose pins found,Production line experienced multiple intermittent DSQC_662 communication errors over two shifts causing unplanned stops. Technician found multiple loose pins in X2 mating block and frayed shield on cable; measured connector torque low at 0.2 Nm and joint contact resistance ~0.95 Ω indicating poor contact. Decision made to replace DSQC 662 module from spares; new card installed and full system functional with stable +24 V rail at 24.1 V.,"Isolated power, removed DSQC-662 card, inspected and documented damage (loose X2 pins, frayed shield). Installed replacement DSQC-662 spare, secured X1/X2, torqued terminals to 0.8 Nm, verified +24 V at PSU terminal, ran 10 cycle motion test on Axis 2/3/4 with no recurrence, tagged failed board for rework.",resolved,142,184,178,IRC5 DSQC-662 loose_pins X2 connector_torque_nm=0.2->0.8 joint_contact_resistance_ohm=0.95Ω replaced_board ACTION=replaced DSQC_662,irc5_dsqc_662_fault,FM-065,sym_dsqc_662_status_led_red,link_replaced_by_embedding,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01"", ""error_code_source"": ""controller_event_log""}"
log_irc5_0033,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1033,2026-02-28T13:33:30Z,2026-02-28T13:37:30Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,tcp_calibration_drift,condition_monitoring,PDM,closed,10,INFO,comp_robot_arm_system,arm gearbox / joint A3,,,tcp_position_drift_mm,1.21,mm,0.5,mm,WO-IRC5-1033,Post-maintenance TCP drift 1.21 mm — bearing service follow-up and recalibration,"After scheduled bearing replacement on joint A3, weekly check showed TCP drift 1.21 mm. During service the A3 gearbox was opened and encoder coupling loosened; found slight axial play on the replaced bearing seating. Drive temp A3 at 56°C after run-in, DC bus 565 V, no alarms in drive log.","Rechecked bearing preload and seating, re-secured encoder coupling (5 Nm), updated revolution counters per instruction, completed four-point TCP calibration then performed fine calibration; ran 30-cycle motion profile and confirmed TCP drift reduced to 0.13 mm. Logged gearbox fitment for closer tolerance next replacement.",resolved,165,158,123,IRC5 arm gearbox joint A3 TCP tcp_position_drift_mm=1.21mm post-bearing_service revcounters updated action:four-point_cal,irc5_robot_tcp_calibration_drift,fm_robot_not_calibrated_correctly,sym_inconsistent_path_accuracy,"date_imputed,link_replaced_by_embedding","{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0060,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1060,2026-03-03T09:20:32Z,2026-03-03T09:21:32Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,bearing_wear_indication,condition_monitoring,PDM,closed,14,WARN,comp_bearing,Axis 2 motor bearing / flange assembly,,,bearing_vibration_rms_mm_s,6.09,mm/s,4.0,mm/s,WO-IRC5-1060,J2 vibration alert resolved — false positive after re-mounting motor flange,"Initial PDM hit showed 6.09 mm/s on axis 2; however on-site inspection found loose motor flange and poorly seated accelerometer cable, not bearing damage. After re-mounting motor, re-torquing flange bolts and re-seating accelerometer, repeat measurement dropped to 1.3 mm/s and no mechanical noise was present — concluded false positive from mounting/measurement issue.","Shutdown axis 2, applied LOTO. Removed motor guard, removed and inspected motor-to-gearbox flange (no pitting on bearing race, grease normal). Cleaned contact faces, fitted new M6 flat washers, re-torqued flange bolts to 30 Nm per spec, re-mounted accelerometer (B&K 4381) with fresh adhesive pad and re-routed cable. Performed three verification runs at 50% and 100% speed; captured post-fix vibration (1.3 mm/s RMS) and returned axis to service. Closed event as resolved — no bearing replacement required.",resolved,90,78,,IRC5 Axis2 bearing false_positive vibration_rms_mm_s=6.09->1.3 mm/s after remount; motor flange retorqued; accelerometer reseated; resolved,irc5_bearings_wear_pdm,fm_bearings_damaged_or_worn,,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0018,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1018,2026-03-04T16:13:48Z,2026-03-04T16:15:48Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,drive_motor_overtemperature,alarm,CM,closed,14,WARN,comp_drive_module,Drive module heatsink (axis4),,,drive_motor_temp_c,89,C,75,C,WO-IRC5-1018,Heatsink fouling on axis 4 — dust layer causing overheating,"Axis 4 drive motor temp recorded 89°C; investigation found ~6 mm dust cake on the drive heatsink and around the shroud causing poor convection. Thermal pad between module and heatsink was brittle and partially delaminated, gearbox surface ~72°C. Fans were spinning and supply voltages normal; accumulation likely from prolonged dry environment.","Powered down and allowed to cool per safety procedure. Removed heatsink shroud, mechanically cleaned dust with vacuum and soft brush, replaced degraded thermal pad and applied fresh thermal interface compound. Reassembled, performed burn-in; drive temp dropped to ~65°C and gearbox cooled to 58°C. Advised monthly clean schedule.",resolved,96,150,78,IRC5 drive module axis4 dusty heatsink drive_motor_temp_c=89C cleaned replaced thermal pad reduced temp,irc5_drive_motor_overtemperature,fm_components_overheated,sym_gearbox_axis_6_getting_hot,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0012,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1012,2026-03-07T07:07:40Z,2026-03-07T07:10:40Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,flexpendant_disconnected,alarm,CM,open,14,WARN,comp_flexpendant,FlexPendant cable (strain relief),,,flexpendant_connected,0,boolean,1,boolean,WO-IRC5-1012,Steady power LED but periodic comm drops — abrasion at strain relief (monitoring),Power LED on pendant remained steady yet IRC5 intermittently reported flexpendant_connected=0; operator log shows short drops approximately every 45–60 minutes. Inspection discovered outer jacket abrasion at strain relief exposing inner conductors but continuity tests passed; temporary repair applied and unit placed on monitoring. Replacement of the full pendant cable recommended because splice is only interim remediation.,"1) Inspected strain relief area, found abrasion ~12 mm long. 2) Performed temporary splice and sealed with self-fusing tape and heat-shrink, added extra strain relief wrap. 3) Verified flexpendant_connected toggled back to 1; placed on continuous monitoring and requested PO for full cable replacement. 4) Logged that alarm remains set to 'monitor'.",monitoring,80,102,26,"IRC5 FlexPendant comm drops, flexpendant_connected=0 intermittent, abrasion at strain relief, temporary splice applied, monitor for replacement",irc5_flexpendant_disconnected,fm_cable_connector_damaged,sym_flexpendant_dead,link_replaced_by_embedding,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0069,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1069,2026-03-10T11:14:48Z,2026-03-10T11:15:48Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,service_tools_left_behind,fault,CM,closed,14,WARN,comp_robot_arm_system,IRC5 arm — wrist/Axis 6 (end-effector flange),,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1069,Allen key left on wrist after calibration — stopped on safety check,"During post-maintenance verification the controller flagged an unexpected TCP offset and entered a safety stop. Technician found a 3mm Allen key lodged between the end-effector flange and Axis 6 cover (calibration jig location) after recent TCP calibration, which altered payload/moment briefly and tripped the check. No scoring on flange; robot responded normally once tool was removed.","Removed Allen key and calibration jig remnants, inspected wrist seal and cable routing, re-zeroed TCP reference via IRC5 guide routine, performed slow dry-run of the teach program to verify path and payload handling, then returned cell to production. Logged corrective action and amended SOP to require physical tool count at sign-off.",resolved,12,33,22,"IRC5; IRC5 arm wrist Axis 6; Allen key left behind; TCP offset / safety stop; removed key, re-zeroed TCP, dry-run verification; SOP/PM update logged",irc5_service_tools_left_behind,fm_robot_not_calibrated_correctly,,link_replaced_by_embedding,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0073,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1073,2026-03-10T16:45:50Z,2026-03-10T16:46:50Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,suspected_esd_damage,fault,CM,closed,18,ERROR,comp_dsqc_604,DSQC 604 PSU board,,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1073,IRC5 random reboots — suspected ESD on DSQC 604 PSU board,Controller experienced unexplained reboots and lost project configuration; event log shows multiple spontaneous resets over ~3.5 hours and servo faults on Axis 2 and Axis 6. Visual inspection of the DSQC 604 PSU area found a small carbonization mark near C12 and intermittent continuity on the ribbon connector; ambient humidity measured low at ~14% (dry winter conditions). Technician confirmed wrist strap had been used earlier in the day but noted it was not tethered to an earth point during a brief bench check.,"Powered down controller following lockout procedures, removed and replaced DSQC 604 assembly with factory spare, verified +24V rail = 24.2 V and +5V rail = 5.05 V at service connector, reseated ribbon cables and cleaned contacts with electronics-grade IPA. Restored controller project from last known-good backup, ran homing and 5-cycle motion test on all axes (no reboots), recorded results and tagged original board for ESD-failure analysis; advised operations to use ESD mat and tethered wrist strap going forward.",resolved,163,231,139,IRC5 DSQC 604 ESD random_reboots lost_configuration Axis2 Axis6 carbonized_C12 +24V=24.2V +5V=5.05V replaced_board restored_project wrist-strap_used RH=14% recommend_ESD_mat,irc5_esd_event,fm_electrostatic_discharge_damages_sensitive_electronics,sym_unit_sensitive_to_esd,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0075,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1075,2026-03-13T07:57:03Z,2026-03-13T07:59:03Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,intermittent_signal_loss,fault,CM,closed,14,WARN,,Panel board TB1 (FlexPendant comm),,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1075,"Intermittent FlexPendant comms — TB1 terminal loose, re-torqued",Operator reported intermittent signal loss from the FlexPendant. Thermal imaging (FLIR) showed a hotspot at the panel-board TB1 terminal strip (58°C vs ambient 22°C). Torque check revealed TB1 screw terminals at ~0.40 Nm (spec 0.60 Nm); screened cable clamp also loosely seated. After re-torquing to 0.60 Nm and reseating the cable screen clamp the errors cleared; monitored for 30 minutes with no recurrence.,"Isolated IRC5, powered down to safe state, removed front cover. Inspected TB1 (FlexPendant comm) — measured terminal torque 0.40 Nm with calibrated torque driver; cleaned oxide on contact surfaces with isopropyl, re-torqued terminals to 0.60 Nm, re-seated and clamped cable screen, applied light film of contact protectant, reassembled and powered up. Verified communication, reviewed event log, monitored for 30 min and closed task.",resolved,104,70,85,"IRC5 Panel board TB1 thermal hotspot 58C, connector_torque_nm 0.40->0.60, cable screen reseated, intermittent comms cleared",irc5_loose_connections_dry_joints,fm_loose_connections_or_dry_joints,,missing_component,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0048,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1048,2026-03-19T21:32:28Z,2026-03-19T21:35:28Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,usb_communication_error,alarm,CM,open,14,WARN,comp_usb_connection,DSQC 662 USB interface (controller CPU rear),,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1048,Persistent pulsing LED after recent update — firmware-related USB enumeration issue (open),"After a recent controller software update the DSQC 662 status LED began pulsing green during backup and the USB device intermittently drops out; error reproducible on two different known-good drives. Voltage on VBUS reads steady 5.06 V and physical ports check OK, suggesting a firmware-level USB enumeration bug rather than hardware failure. Issue remains under monitoring while logs are being analyzed and a vendor ticket is open.","Captured system logs (trace log, event dump), recorded controller SW build and patch level, performed controlled repro with USB sniffer capture on VBUS and D+/D- during backup, swapped ports and drives to isolate hardware (both OK). Put controller in maintenance mode and escalated to ABB support (SR# 20260319-ABB), left machine available for production with scheduled supervised backups. Monitoring for recurrence and awaiting vendor firmware patch.",monitoring,33,34,10,IRC5 DSQC662 USB_interface pulsing_green dsqc662_status_led usb_connected=intermittent firmware_bug logs_captured escalated=SR# monitoring,irc5_usb_communication_error,fm_usb_communication_error,sym_dsqc_662_status_led_pulsing_green,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0067,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1067,2026-03-20T13:06:09Z,2026-03-20T13:10:09Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,gearbox_oil_temperature_high,alarm,CM,open,18,ERROR,comp_gearboxes,"Gearbox, Axis 6 (output flange)",,,gearbox_oil_temp_c,100,C,85,C,WO-IRC5-1067,"Axis 6 gearbox critical overtemp — 100°C, isolated for inspection",Axis 6 gearbox oil reached 100°C (thresh 85°C) and held for 94 minutes during heavy-duty operation; ambient in cell ~40°C and gearbox surface >85°C on IR spot check. Sight glass showed nominal level but oil smelled slightly burnt; no external leaks but notable varnish film inside breather cap. Placed axis in maintenance stop and entered monitoring state pending further teardown.,Enabled MAINT stop for Axis 6 and reduced all production duty cycles; deployed forced external cooling (fans + portable AC directed at cabinet); took oil sample (2 mL) and sent to lab for viscosity/oxidation; scheduled gearbox teardown and bearing inspection for next shift; set continuous temp logging and alarm if >90°C.,monitoring,125,94,51,IRC5 Gearbox Axis6 gearbox_oil_temp_c=100C critical overtemp ambient40C oil_sample_taken duty_cycle_reduced MAINT_stop,irc5_gearbox_oil_temperature_high,fm_gearbox_oil_too_hot,sym_gearbox_axis_6_getting_hot,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0071,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1071,2026-03-20T20:49:17Z,2026-03-20T20:50:17Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,io_update_interval_too_low,alarm,CM,closed,18,ERROR,comp_io_units,Fieldbus I/O units (PLC-side profile after firmware bump),,,io_update_interval_ms,28,ms,20,ms,WO-IRC5-1071,Post-PLC firmware bump: I/O update interval > threshold (28ms) — tuned intervals,"Following a PLC firmware bump earlier in the day the controller alarmed with io_update_interval_ms = 28 ms (threshold 20 ms) and short program stalls reported. PLC scan time analysis showed the PLC had reduced its internal poll spacing (effective PLC scan ~4–6 ms) after the firmware change, increasing I/O traffic to the IRC5 and pushing io bus load to ~74%. Observed CPU load on IRC5 rose to ~63% during peak cycles.","Coordinated with controls: reverted PLC I/O scan parameters to previous profile where possible and adjusted offending I/O units in RobotStudio — increased several update intervals to 40–200 ms and converted unused status bits to change-of-state. Performed controlled restart of PLC and IRC5 communications, validated steady state: io_update_interval_ms settled at ~18–22ms average and bus load dropped to ~49%. Documented firmware change and configuration tuning; production downtime ~14 min.",resolved,113,174,14,IRC5 Fieldbus I/O units io_update_interval_ms=28ms PLC firmware bump PLC_scan_time=4-6ms RobotStudio adjust intervals,irc5_io_update_interval_too_low,fm_io_update_interval_too_low,,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0006,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1006,2026-03-22T21:34:50Z,2026-03-22T21:38:50Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,ethernet_packet_loss,alarm,CM,open,18,ERROR,comp_communications,IRC5 controller Ethernet port (front RJ45),,,ethernet_packet_loss_pct,5.23,%,2.0,%,WO-IRC5-1006,Controller NIC link going down intermittently under load,5.23% packet loss recorded while IRC5 executing heavy motion programs. Network diagnostics showed controller NIC reporting link state transitions to DOWN for short intervals; no visible damage to cable or connectors. Switch logs show transient link flaps from the IRC5 MAC address at high CPU/load times.,"Power-cycled IRC5 controller network stack, forced link to 100Mb/Full on switch port to stabilize, upgraded NIC driver on controller to latest approved revision, and enabled port storm-filtering. Placed on continued monitoring (open) with recommendation to replace controller NIC if flaps persist beyond 72 hours.",monitoring,149,183,51,IRC5 controller NIC link-flap ethernet_packet_loss_pct=5.23% action: forced 100Mb/full & upgraded NIC driver; link DOWN events observed,irc5_communications_ethernet_packet_loss,fm_ethernet_network_has_problems,,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0053,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1053,2026-03-25T06:52:55Z,2026-03-25T06:53:55Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,joystick_anomaly,fault,CM,open,10,INFO,comp_joystick,FlexPendant joystick (thermal-sensitive FP unit),,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1053,Joystick drift after warm-up; monitoring in place,After several minutes of production the joystick center slowly drifted and required re-centering. Ambient around pendant rose from 18°C to ~28°C; FP internal temp reported 46°C and ADC center shifted +0.12 V when warm. Issue reproduces with temperature change but has not yet caused a production stop.,"Noted and started monitoring. Moved pendant away from heat source (cooling fan and insulating spacer fitted), performed temporary recalibration to reduce immediate drift, and logged event for scheduled replacement of joystick PCB during next planned PM. Continued production with periodic checks.",monitoring,90,50,46,IRC5 FlexPendant joystick thermal drift ADC+0.12V @46°C temp rise 18→28°C moved pendant insulated recalibrated scheduled replacement,irc5_joystick_anomaly,fm_joystick_malfunctioning,sym_flexpendant_dead,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0050,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1050,2026-03-25T16:41:07Z,2026-03-25T16:45:07Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,mains_power_loss,fault,CM,closed,18,ERROR,,IRC5 control cabinet — F2 fuse / T1 transformer area,2002,F2_OPEN,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1050,"Local mains feed fault — F2 blown, T1 secondary lost, controller offline","Operator reported controller dropped out while plant power was normal. Incoming mains measured 400 VAC L-L at the cabinet and main switch was ON, but F2 fuse was open on continuity check; F6 breaker was closed and reset OK. Transformer T1 primary showed 400 VAC but secondary measured 0 VAC due to the open F2 feed; no voltage at control module service outlet. Replaced F2 (2 A, 250 VAC fast-blow spare from cabinet), verified T1 secondary restored to ~24 VAC, cycled mains, cleared event and confirmed controller boot and axis home OK.","Locked out and isolated; measured mains at mains terminals = 400 VAC; inspected main switch (ON) and F6 (not tripped); removed and ohmed F2 — open circuit; installed replacement F2 2A/250V fuse from spare kit; rechecked continuity and T1 secondary (restored ~24 VAC); powered up controller, cleared alarm, verified service outlet 230 VAC and USB link to laptop, ran smoke-test and returned unit to production.",resolved,114,165,122,"IRC5 F2 blown, controller mains feed fault, mains_voltage_vac 400 VAC at input, F2 open -> T1 secondary 0V then restored to 24VAC after replacement, replaced F2, powered controller, cleared alarm",irc5_mains_power_supply_loss,FM-072,,"missing_component,link_replaced_by_embedding","{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0027,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1027,2026-03-26T10:58:10Z,2026-03-26T11:02:10Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,main_computer_event_log_errors,fault,CM,open,14,WARN,comp_main_computer,Main computer (controller CPU) - IRC5,30102,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1027,"IRC5 main computer — intermittent errors post-firmware update, under monitoring","Event log contains mixed errors across communications, I/O and system layers (approx. 220 entries/24h) appearing gradually since last scheduled firmware update three days ago. Predominant entries: I/O module A timeout, FlexPendant communication lost (no image), and occasional system watchdog entries. CPU LED currently green but errors recur during load peaks; event pattern suggests regression after firmware change rather than hardware supply failure (main_computer_supply_vdc steady at 24.0 VDC).","Collected full diagnostics bundle (eventlog, .TAR system dump, fw_version.txt) and exported to USB, attempted controlled rollback to previous firmware image in maintenance window (rolled back but errors persisted intermittently). ABB TAC contacted and case opened; system left in monitoring with reduced production profile and scheduled for deeper analysis.",monitoring,90,148,60,"IRC5 main computer; event_log_error_count=220/24h; FlexPendant comms lost, I/O timeout, main_computer_supply_vdc=24.0V, fw_update fragment; collected diag, attempted fw rollback, monitoring",irc5_main_computer_event_log_error_count_high,fm_flexpendant_power_supply_from_controller_faulty,sym_erratic_event_messages_on_flexpendant,link_replaced_by_embedding,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0013,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1013,2026-03-31T10:32:01Z,2026-03-31T10:32:01Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,flexpendant_disconnected,alarm,CM,closed,18,ERROR,comp_flexpendant,FlexPendant RJ45 plug (corroded pin),,,flexpendant_connected,0,boolean,1,boolean,WO-IRC5-1013,Emergency stop triggered by pendant disconnect; cleaned corroded RJ45 pin,"Pendant intermittently lost connection causing an E-stop during a shift; pendant screen showed comm error and power LED was unstable. Cabinet inspection revealed light corrosion on RJ45 pin 2 and poor contact resulting in intermittent open; pendant cable showed moisture staining. Cleaned contacts with contact cleaner, re-crimped plug, replaced the rubber boot and verified 24.1 V at DSQC-662 before returning to service.","1) Isolated system and removed pendant. 2) Disassembled RJ45 plug, cleaned pins with IPA and contact cleaner, re-crimped new plug, installed new strain relief boot. 3) Measured DSQC-662 feed = 24.1 V and continuity across cable pins. 4) Reconnected, cleared E-stop, ran 45 min verification with no disconnects observed.",resolved,44,33,32,"IRC5 FlexPendant disconnect caused E-stop, flexpendant_connected=0, RJ45 pin corrosion, cleaned and re-crimped, 24.1V verified",irc5_flexpendant_disconnected,fm_flexpendant_cable_damaged,sym_flexpendant_starts_but_does_not_communicate_with_controller,link_replaced_by_embedding,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0057,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1057,2026-03-31T13:51:38Z,2026-03-31T13:55:38Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,flexpendant_cable_damaged,fault,CM,closed,14,WARN,comp_flexpendant_cable,FlexPendant cable - connector strain relief (cut),,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1057,Small cut at connector strain relief causing intermittent pendant power; replaced cable,"Tech observed intermittent pendant downtime and found a shallow lateral cut in the cable jacket at the connector strain relief (approx. 20 mm from plug) causing intermittent 24 V under flex. Continuity while flexing produced open intermittently on the pendant power conductor. Replaced entire FlexPendant cable assembly, fitted new rubber grommet and strain relief, and verified stable pendant voltage and CAN communications.","1) Isolated controller power and removed teach pendant. 2) Performed wiggle test with DMM and observed intermittent open on 24 V conductor; recorded intermittent behavior. 3) Installed new FlexPendant cable assembly (stock spare), torqued connector shell per manual, installed new strain relief gasket, powered up and confirmed steady 24.2 V and normal pendant operation. 4) Entered work order and flagged old cable for disposal and inspection.",resolved,72,180,43,IRC5 FlexPendant cable connector strain relief cut; intermittent 24V open during flex; replaced cable assembly; verified 24.2V and pendant comms,irc5_flexpendant_cable_damaged,fm_flexpendant_cable_damaged,sym_flexpendant_starts_but_does_not_communicate_with_controller,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0074,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1074,2026-04-01T08:18:42Z,2026-04-01T08:22:42Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,suspected_esd_damage,fault,CM,closed,14,WARN,comp_dsqc_604,DSQC 604 daughterboard (I/O ribbon),,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1074,WARN: intermittent CRC/NV errors — suspected ESD upset to DSQC 604,"After prior service the unit began throwing sporadic CRC and NV checksum warnings and produced occasional axis 3 position errors during a production run; no permanent smoke or heat signatures observed. Technician measured wrist strap continuity at ~1.0 MΩ (strap present and worn during maintenance) and ambient RH ~16%, but no dissipative table or floor mat was in use at the bench.","Checked event log and saved fault dump, measured earth reference (<1 Ω) and strap continuity, reseated DSQC 604 daughterboard ribbon and I/O connectors, applied contact cleaner, restored project from server backup and saved a fresh backup to local archive. Deployed a temporary dissipative table mat and grounded wrist strap tether for remainder of shift, performed 10-cycle verification program on axis 3 and recorded no further CRC warnings; recommended permanent ESD mat and revised handling steps to operations.",resolved,144,202,180,IRC5 DSQC 604 CRC_error NV_checksum axis3_fault strap_cont=1.0MΩ RH=16% reseated_ribbon restored_project deployed_dissipative_mat,irc5_esd_event,FM-069,sym_wrong_input_voltage_dsqc_604,link_replaced_by_embedding,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0034,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1034,2026-04-03T06:02:29Z,2026-04-03T06:03:29Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,tcp_calibration_drift,condition_monitoring,PDM,closed,14,WARN,comp_robot_arm_system,robot arm system (A2 encoder coupling),3110,IRQ-3110,tcp_position_drift_mm,2.03,mm,0.5,mm,WO-IRC5-1034,"Gradual TCP drift 2.03 mm — worn coupling, recalibration and part ordered","Weekly calibration recorded TCP drift 2.03 mm. No recent impacts reported; inspection revealed wear on A2 encoder coupling (visible keyway play ~0.6 mm) and slight backlash audible at low speed, suggesting mechanical wear. Joint temps A1-A3 trending high (A2 59°C), drive DC link 558 V, path accuracy inconsistent during verification moves.","Isolated cell, replaced encoder coupling with spare, re-torqued encoder clamp, performed revolution counter check and updated values, executed four-point and fine calibration, then validated path accuracy (repeatability 0.12 mm). Raised work order to replace worn coupling assembly and scheduled full gearbox inspection; production returned after verification.",resolved,136,187,159,IRC5 robot arm system A2 coupling TCP tcp_position_drift_mm=2.03mm worn_coupling ERR3110 action:replace_coupling+four-point_cal,irc5_robot_tcp_calibration_drift,fm_robot_not_calibrated_correctly,sym_inconsistent_path_accuracy,link_replaced_by_embedding,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0019,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1019,2026-04-03T07:10:34Z,2026-04-03T07:10:34Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,drive_motor_overtemperature,alarm,CM,open,18,ERROR,comp_drive_module,Drive module M3 (axis3),7200,,drive_motor_temp_c,84,C,75,C,WO-IRC5-1019,Axis 3 overtemp after production recipe change — monitoring,"After a recipe change, axis 3 drive_motor_temp_c rose to 84°C (threshold 75°C) and remained elevated for 112 minutes. New profile increases acceleration and holds torque longer; no fan or filter faults found and cabinet ambient 34°C. Technician observed increased current draw on M3 during peak moves but no immediate hardware fault.",Reduced peak accel and shortened dwell times in the program as an interim measure and instructed operators to run a cooldown cycle between batches. Installed additional temp logging on axis 3 (sample every 30 s) and left the unit under monitoring while production continues at reduced rate; plan thermal duty-cycle study next week.,monitoring,160,112,20,IRC5 drive module axis3 high duty cycle drive_motor_temp_c=84C reduced accel added logging monitoring,irc5_drive_motor_overtemperature,fm_components_overheated,sym_gearbox_axis_6_getting_hot,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0054,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1054,2026-04-04T09:33:22Z,2026-04-04T09:37:22Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,joystick_anomaly,fault,CM,closed,14,WARN,comp_joystick,FP joystick cable / 8-pin connector (pin 4),,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1054,Intermittent joystick input loss traced to connector continuity,"During a run the joystick intermittently stopped sending input (jog commands ignored), then recovered without operator action. Multimeter continuity check on the 8‑pin pendant cable showed intermittent open on pin 4 under light flex; visual inspection showed fretting at the pin and slight deformation of the crimp.","Put controller in manual, powered down pendant and replaced the flex cable and mating connector housing. Cleaned mating face, re-terminated connector, power-cycled pendant and drive module, and re-calibrated joystick. Completed 30-minute soak test with repeated jog commands — no recurrence.",resolved,83,68,36,IRC5 FlexPendant joystick intermittent input connector pin4 continuity fault replaced cable re-terminated power-cycled soak test OK,irc5_joystick_anomaly,fm_flexpendant_cable_damaged,sym_flexpendant_starts_but_does_not_communicate_with_controller,link_replaced_by_embedding,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0076,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1076,2026-04-05T13:28:34Z,2026-04-05T13:31:34Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,intermittent_signal_loss,fault,CM,closed,18,ERROR,comp_panel_board,Panel board CN5 (comm),,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1076,Controller comm board CN5 — intermittent errors; dry joint found and repaired,"Multiple intermittent errors and a single ERR state occurred during run. Infrared scan showed elevated temperature at CN5 connector area (~62°C), suggesting a high-resistance joint. Visual/tactile inspection found a slightly raised solder fillet and a loose screw terminal (measured at ~0.30 Nm). Technician reflowed the suspect solder joint, re-torqued screw to 0.50 Nm (spec 0.50 Nm), re-clamped the screen braid and confirmed stable communications; logged event and returned unit to production.","Shutdown and earth-grounded controller. Removed board cover, inspected CN5 (comm board) and solder side — performed light solder rework on one dry joint using 350°C iron and flux, cleaned residue. Measured screw terminal torque (0.30 Nm) and tightened to spec 0.50 Nm with calibrated driver. Reattached cable screens, performed continuity and low-resistance check across connector (post-repair contact resistance improved), power cycled and validated operation. Monitored logs for 2 production cycles then closed.",resolved,61,154,13,"IRC5 Panel board CN5 dry joint thermal 62C, joint_contact_resistance_ohm reduced after reflow, connector_torque_nm 0.30->0.50, reflowed and re-clamped screen",irc5_loose_connections_dry_joints,fm_loose_connections_or_dry_joints,,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0023,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1023,2026-04-09T08:46:12Z,2026-04-09T08:46:12Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,brake_release_fault,fault,CM,open,18,ERROR,comp_robot_brakes,Axis 3 brake cable / TB12 harness,,,brake_release_voltage_vdc,18.14,VDC,24,VDC,WO-IRC5-1023,Axis 3 brake supply cable open; temporary test confirms coil OK — awaiting harness replacement,"Axis 3 would not release; brake_release_voltage_vdc measured at 18.14 VDC (well below 24 VDC). Secured arm with an external safety lock before removing the lower panel; continuity to the brake was open at the panel terminal block (infinite Ω) while isolated bench test of the brake coil showed 47.9 Ω and the coil operated when fed a regulated 24 V supply. Found a burned/chafed conductor at TB12 in the cabinet — cable failure suspected. Temporarily tagged the circuit, left the axis secured, and scheduled harness replacement; monitoring state remains open pending parts.","Secured arm with rated external lockout fixture; isolated servo and controller power; measured brake_release_voltage = 18.14 V; continuity check from TB12 to brake = open/infinite; removed connector and bench-tested coil with external 24 V supply (coil R = 47.9 Ω and brake released); documented burn on conductor at TB12, applied temporary safety tag and cable protection, logged parts request for replacement harness and left system in safe state for scheduled repair (monitoring).",monitoring,217,148,83,IRC5 Axis 3 brake cable fault; brake_release_voltage_vdc=18.14V; coil_res=47.9Ω (bench OK); TB12 conductor burned/open; secured arm and scheduled harness replacement,irc5_brake_release_fault,fm_holding_brakes_disconnected_faulty_worn_or_non_operational,sym_holding_brakes_non_operational,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0041,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1041,2026-04-10T19:57:46Z,2026-04-10T20:01:46Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,pm_brake_inspection,maintenance,PM,completed,10,INFO,comp_robot_brakes,IRC5 brake system (sampled axes),,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1041,PM brake functional verification — all axes quick check,"Completed follow-up PM brake verification across IRC5 after prior service entries. Random sampling of linings showed average thickness ~2.0 mm, coil release voltage 24.2 V and ambient temp ~30°C. Full controller brake routine executed with no alarms and normal timing. Records updated and unit returned to production.","Ran full brake routine from teach pendant, spot-checked lining thickness with calipers, verified coil voltage under nominal load, closed panels and captured photos for PM record; updated CMMS entry.",resolved,142,157,146,"IRC5, Robot brakes sampled, lining thickness 2.0mm, coil voltage 24.2V, brake routine OK, photos and CMMS updated",irc5_pm_brake_inspection,FM-059,sym_holding_brakes_non_operational,link_inferred_by_embedding,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0064,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1064,2026-04-11T08:19:30Z,2026-04-11T08:21:30Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,operator_note_vibration,operator_note,,closed,10,INFO,,Tool flange / TCP mount,,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1064,Brief TCP vibration during quick traverse — operator note,Operator felt a short vibration at the tool centerpoint (TCP) during a quick traverse between pick/place points. Vibration only when speed exceeded ~1.5 m/s; tool flange and mounting inspected with no detectable axial/play. DC bus reading checked at the cabinet ~48.1 Vdc and motor temp ~46 °C; no alarms recorded and event was cleared after note. Logged and closed — production continued.,"noted, continued production",,,13,10,"IRC5 | Tool flange (TCP mount) | brief vibration at >1.5 m/s, DC bus ~48.1 Vdc, motor temp 46°C, no alarms | noted, continued production",irc5_operator_note_unknown_vibration,,,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0007,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1007,2026-04-11T15:53:55Z,2026-04-11T15:53:55Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,ethernet_packet_loss,alarm,CM,open,18,ERROR,comp_communications,Distribution Switch SFP (uplink to IRC5 VLAN),,,ethernet_packet_loss_pct,6.23,%,2.0,%,WO-IRC5-1007,Switch SFP/transceiver dirty or failing causing packet errors,6.23% packet loss noted on plant backbone between IRC5 and main PLC. Inspection of SFP on distribution switch showed dust and slight burn discoloration on transceiver faceplate; SFP error counters show increasing FEC/CRC errors. Controller link occasionally showed carrier-loss messages but stayed UP most of the time.,"Pulled SFP, cleaned with lint-free swab and isopropyl alcohol, re-seated and then replaced SFP with tested spare when errors persisted. After replacing transceiver, monitored 90 minutes: packet loss decreased to 0.15% and CRC errors cleared. Logged transceiver as failed and quarantined for vendor return.",monitoring,98,193,85,IRC5 Communications switch-SFP CRC-errors ethernet_packet_loss_pct=6.23% action: cleaned then replaced SFP; reduced packet loss,irc5_communications_ethernet_packet_loss,fm_ethernet_network_has_problems,,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0030,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1030,2026-04-17T09:29:06Z,2026-04-17T09:33:06Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,dsqc_662_fault,fault,CM,open,18,ERROR,,DSQC_662 power supply board,662,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1030,"DSQC 662 fault recurring — reseated X1, monitoring in place",DSQC_662 alarm returned during start-up; intermittent nature so left controller in monitoring after basic fixes. Found slight intermittent continuity on X1 screen and connector not fully seated; contact resistance measured ~0.28 Ω and ambient cabinet temp 30°C. Reseated and secured cabling and will escalate to scheduled replacement if fault reappears.,"Soft-power cycle controller, opened cabinet, reseated X1 and X2, tied cable screen to chassis ground, tightened connector screws to ~0.5 Nm, applied small amount of anti-oxidant on contacts, and restarted system. Logged event for follow-up and set monitoring flag; planned spare DSQC_662 replacement if next fault occurs.",monitoring,116,122,178,IRC5 DSQC_662 X1 reseat intermittent continuity joint_contact_resistance_ohm=0.28Ω monitoring plan replace_if_repeat ACTION=reseated X1/X2,irc5_dsqc_662_fault,FM-067,SYM-025,"missing_component,link_replaced_by_embedding","{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01"", ""error_code_source"": ""controller_event_log""}"
log_irc5_0072,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1072,2026-04-18T20:49:54Z,2026-04-18T20:51:54Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,io_update_interval_too_low,alarm,CM,open,14,WARN,comp_io_units,"Combined I/O units (digital I/O bus, mixed legacy modules)",,,io_update_interval_ms,29,ms,20,ms,WO-IRC5-1072,Monitoring: elevated I/O update interval observed (29ms) — follow-up scheduled,IRC5 alarm persists showing io_update_interval_ms = 29 ms (threshold 20 ms); condition recurring intermittently over last 69 minutes with several short program slowdowns. Fieldbus load trending high (60–70%) and IRC5 CPU load averaging ~68% during peak product changeovers. No single root cause isolated on initial pass — likely combination of high cyclic traffic and a few noisy legacy I/O modules.,"Completed initial fieldbus load review and moved low-priority sensor groups to change-of-state in RobotStudio (minimal scope change to avoid production impact). Enabled high-frequency logging (1 min) for io_update_interval_ms, io_bus_load_pct and cpu_load_pct; created follow-up work package to review remaining legacy modules on next scheduled shutdown and to coordinate deeper PLC scan profiling. Continued production with monitoring; downtime while making safe changes ~34 min.",monitoring,92,69,34,IRC5 Combined I/O units io_update_interval_ms=29ms io_bus_load=60-70% monitoring enabled RobotStudio COS changes scheduled,irc5_io_update_interval_too_low,fm_io_update_interval_too_low,,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0077,mock_cmms,WO-IRC5-1077,2026-04-22T20:04:28Z,2026-04-22T20:08:28Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,intermittent_signal_loss,fault,CM,open,14,WARN,comp_panel_board,Panel board — screen clamp / emergency chain,,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1077,Ongoing intermittent signal loss — screen clamp & some terminals borderline,Intermittent signal faults persist intermittently; technician observed minor heating on the screen clamp area (46°C) during a 54 min observation window. Torque spot-check found one terminal at ~0.50 Nm (spec 0.60 Nm) and two others acceptable; cables on the emergency/enable chain were snugged and screen re-terminated but intermittent alarms still appear sporadically. Condition left in monitoring state with plan to replace suspect terminal block and schedule extended thermal/log capture.,"Performed live inspection with safety lockout in place, recorded thermal images, and checked torque on panel board terminals — tightened borderline terminal from ~0.50 Nm to 0.60 Nm with calibrated torque wrench. Re-terminated cable screen clamps, applied additional strain relief and zip-tied harness to reduce movement. Escalated to parts for replacement terminal block (order raised), enabled continuous event logging and scheduled follow-up in 72 hours for extended monitoring.",monitoring,69,54,51,"IRC5 Panel board screen clamp thermal 46C, connector_torque_nm 0.50->0.60, intermittent_errors monitoring, re-terminated screen and scheduled block replacement",irc5_loose_connections_dry_joints,fm_loose_connections_or_dry_joints,,,"{""raw_equipment_id"": ""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0078,IRC5_event_log,IRC5-CTRL-01-EV-20240110-131816-567,2024-01-10T13:18:16Z,2024-01-10T13:28:28Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,dsqc_662_fault,fault,CM,closed,22,FATAL,comp_dsqc_662,DSQC 662 drive board,10182,,,,,,,,DSQC 662 reset during cycle,DSQC 662 fault logged with matching drive supply warning. Harness moved slightly when cabinet door opened.,Tightened board retaining screws and verified cooling airflow.,resolved,150,103,75,IRC5 DSQC 662 board fault code 10182 10183 connector LEDs drive supply action: reseat monitor,irc5_dsqc_662_fault,,,unmapped_failure_mode,"{""cell"":""A"",""line"":""welding""}"
log_irc5_0079,manual_logbook,OPLOG-2024-Q1-4568,2024-01-10T14:37:15Z,2024-01-10T14:52:27Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,operator_note_vibration,operator_note,,open,10,INFO,comp_robot_arm_system,manipulator base,,,,,,,,,Pendant flicker again,"Robot felt 'jerky' on long traverse this AM. Op B. says probably fine, no alarm shown.","No action, no alarm active.",monitoring,,,,IRC5 operator note unknown vibration jerky hum no alarm action: noted monitor,irc5_operator_note_unknown_vibration,,,,"{""shift"":""day"",""operator"":""L.R.""}"
log_irc5_0080,IRC5_event_log,IRC5-CTRL-01-EV-20240111-183437-569,2024-01-11T18:34:37Z,2024-01-11T18:37:44Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,safemove_violation,fault,,closed,18,ERROR,comp_robot_arm_system,SafeMove2 monitored zone,,71046,,,,,,,SafeMove zone violation on fixture edge,SafeMove2 stop occurred at cell entry. Operator was in reduced speed; no collision marks.,Adjusted path point away from boundary and saved backup.,resolved,,,,IRC5 SafeMove2 violation alarm 71046 monitored zone tool data action: correct path backup,irc5_safemove_violation,,,,"{""firmware"":""RobotWare 6.13"",""backup_id"":""BKP-2024-01-11""}"
log_irc5_0081,SAP_PM,000102574570,2024-01-18T12:18:02Z,2024-01-18T12:19:32Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,singularity_configuration_error,alarm,CM,open,10,INFO,comp_robot_arm_system,wrist ax4-6 configuration,,50097,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1078,Singularity warning near wrist flip,Wrist ax4-6 near singularity after new gripper angle. Operator notes slow motion only.,Adjusted approach orientation and added intermediate point.,resolved,30,25,20,IRC5 singularity configuration warning 50097 wrist ax4 ax5 ax6 confdata action: adjust orientation point,irc5_singularity_configuration_error,,,,"{""firmware"":""RobotWare 6.15"",""backup_id"":""BKP-2024-01-18""}"
log_irc5_0082,IRC5_event_log,IRC5-CTRL-01-EV-20240118-181412-571,2024-01-18T18:14:12Z,2024-01-18T18:19:16Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,usb_communication_error,alarm,,open,10,INFO,comp_usb_connection,USB service port / memory stick,,,,,,,,,USB copy failed on RW backup,USB memory stick failed to mount on service port during backup. Second stick mounted normally; port has minor looseness.,Cleaned service port and repeated backup via network share.,monitoring,,,,IRC5 USB communication backup service port memory stick action: use spare media network share,irc5_usb_communication_error,,,,"{""cell"":""A"",""line"":""welding""}"
log_irc5_0083,SAP_PM,000102574572,2024-01-22T10:46:02Z,2024-01-22T10:56:05Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,operator_note_vibration,operator_note,,closed,10,INFO,,unknown,,,,,,,,WO-IRC5-1079,"Slight hum from cab, gone later","Robot felt 'jerky' on long traverse this AM. Op B. says probably fine, no alarm shown.","Noted in logbook, keep running.",resolved,,,,IRC5 operator note unknown vibration jerky hum no alarm action: noted monitor,irc5_operator_note_unknown_vibration,,,,"{""raw_equipment_id"":""IRC5-CTRL-01"",""source"":""sap_pm""}"
log_irc5_0084,SAP_PM,000102574573,2024-01-22T18:54:32Z,2024-01-22T18:54:58Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,pm_brake_inspection,maintenance,PM,completed,10,INFO,comp_robot_brakes,Holding brakes ax5,,,axis_drop_motors_off_mm,0.7,mm,1.5,mm,WO-IRC5-1080,"PM brake check ax5, drop 0.7 mm","Brake PM complete for ax5. Wear mark close to next service line, no immediate stop required.","Checked coil resistance and holding force, no parts changed.",resolved,120,127,60,IRC5 robot brakes PM ax5 axis_drop_motors_off_mm=0.7mm lining wear action: inspect clean trend,irc5_pm_brake_inspection,,,,"{""raw_equipment_id"":""IRC5-CTRL-01"",""source"":""sap_pm""}"
log_irc5_0085,IRC5_event_log,IRC5-CTRL-01-EV-20240127-094123-574,2024-01-27T09:41:23Z,2024-01-27T09:46:35Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,flexpendant_disconnected,alarm,,closed,14,WARN,comp_flexpendant_cable,FlexPendant cable / RJ45 strain relief,10182,,flexpendant_connected,0,boolean,1,boolean,,FlexPendant drop-out on jog screen,"FP disconnect alarm repeated twice this shift. Robot stayed safe, but operator lost jog command during setup.",Reseated pendant cable and added strain relief wrap.,resolved,90,51,0,IRC5 FlexPendant disconnect flexpendant_connected=0 pendant cable RJ45 JOG action: reseat cable strain relief,irc5_flexpendant_disconnected,,,,"{""raw_equipment_id"":""IRC5-CTRL-01""}"
log_irc5_0086,local_plc_scada,PLC-CELLA-ROBOT-20240201-575,2024-02-01T11:40:11Z,2024-02-01T11:45:31Z,irc5-default-instance,asset_irc5,691e2a2a29b1e258a42771f9,IRC5-CTRL-01,cell-a,dsqc_662_fault,alarm,CM,closed,14,WARN,comp_dsqc_662,DSQC 662 drive board,10182,,,,,,,,Drive board DSQC 662 comm fault,"DSQC 662 reported intermittent board fault during cycle transition. LEDs returned green after controller restart, but alarm repeats under high load.","Power cycled cabinet, pulled logs, monitored through batch.",resolved,60,49,0,IRC5 DSQC 662 board fault code 10182 10183 connector LEDs drive supply action: reseat monitor,irc5_dsqc_662_fault,,,,"{""shift"":""late"",""operator"":""L.R.""}"
